                    new("V2.1 boundary program complete", "The V2.1 origin program is COMPLETE (R_001): the final irreducible boundary set has FIVE items — {Difference, η}, {Z2-paired (complex) sector}, {3 octave families}, {SU(2) gauge + j=1/2}, {v, m_e}. 20 objects DERIVED (pairing, complex states, p=3, N=96, span, ΩΛ/Ωm, v structure, M_Pl/v), 10 EMERGENT, 0 OPEN. The anchors v (structure 137·ln span derived, GeV unit boundary) and m_e (pure boundary) are independent of the cosmological density (ΩΛ/Ωm dimensionless fractions only).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-R_001"]),
                    new("V2.1 release ready", "The V2.1 boundary program is tagged-ready (R_002): release notes (Docs/Publication/RELEASE_NOTES_V2_1.md), changelog [2.1.0] — 2026-08-30, tag v2.1-boundary-program, no migration (canonical AT V2.0 unchanged), 372 tests passing.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-R_002"]),
                    new("V2.2 roadmap", "Top-10 V2.2 targets (NP_001) ranked by novelty × testability × V2.1-dependence: T1 measurement origin (D_037 basis + D_039 identity + QG73 collapse), T2 information-encoded measurement, T3 baryon asymmetry (CP QG166), T4 neutrino hierarchy (P2 pending), T5 CMB spectrum, T6 dark-matter status, T7 v/m_e reducibility, T8 Λ coincidence, T9 collapse/observer, T10 initial conditions. Constraints: no new primitives; five-item boundary set respected.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_001"]),
                    new("Eight new predictions", "ResearchY yields 8 predictions absent from V2.0 (D_046): P1 spectral doublets O(2)-type not SU(2); P2 su(2) from unitarity; P3 N=96 selected not closure-produced (only 96 is a zero-defect octave rung); P4 ω₁≈√91·(2π/N); P5 span = N-specific π-analogue; P6 v = 137·ln(span) = 254.37 GeV; P7 v/m_e irreducible; P8 families = floor(log₂ span)+1 = 3. Each has a falsification path; no canonical change.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_046"]),
                    new("A degeneracy is a lock", "The DEGENERACY-LOCK THEOREM (D_047): a symmetry-breaking perturbation of ANY amplitude ε > 0 releases exactly ΔE_lock = (1/N)·Σ_{m_i>1} m_i·ln m_i nats and up to ΔA_max = N − A₀ attractors — independently of ε, because full resolution makes every multiplicity 1 and E₁ = ln N. D96 (42×2,[5],[6]): 0.80231 nats, 51 attractors; K96 (95-fold): 4.50644, 94; random (all-singleton): 0. So G = Δ/ε ∝ 1/ε → DIVERGENT amplification (G_A(D96) = 2.6e10 at ε=1e-9 vs 5.1e4 at ε=1e-3). ΔE_lock DERIVED (closed form); the step response EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_047"]),
                    new("Only structure amplifies", "Amplification is confined to degeneracy-indexed observables (D_047). The continuous spectrum is NOT amplified: G_λ = Δλ_rms/ε is finite and ε-independent, 0.0562 for D96 vs 0.0523 for the degeneracy-free random control — they agree to ~7 %, so the effect is purely degeneracy-driven. REFUTED: 'D96 amplifies weak perturbations in its continuous spectrum'.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_047"]),
                    new("Random is an exact null", "A spectrum with no degeneracy has no lock (D_047): the sparse random graph keeps A ≡ 96 and ΔA ≡ ΔE ≡ 0 with G ≡ 0 for every ε up to 1.0 — its minimum level spacing 0.0123 is an ε-independent barrier no first-order perturbation can remove.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_047"]),
                    new("D96³ amplifies hardest but is capped", "The tensor-product cube D96³ (96³ = 884 736 modes) has the largest reservoir and peaks at G_A ≈ 3.5e12 (≳ 2000× D96), but the unlock saturates at 136 005 of 868 656 = 15.7 % of headroom (D_047): a factor-wise perturbation leaves the factor-permutation symmetry exact, so every permuted triple sum λ_i+λ_j+λ_k stays exactly degenerate. Entropy recovers 57.5 % because the unsplit orbits carry large multiplicities.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-D_047"]),
                    new("Latent degeneracy is a ceiling, not a predictor", "The latent fraction L = (N − A₀)/N bounds the reachable attractor increase (ΔA ≤ N − A₀ by construction). Across six 96-node graphs, the RAW gains scale with L — ρ(ΔA,L) = +0.429, ρ(ΔE,L) = +0.829 — but the NORMALIZED adaptive capacity ΔA/(N − A₀) is uncorrelated with it (ρ = −0.029) even though L spans 0 → 0.98 (D_048). Complete (L = 0.979) has the largest reservoir and the LOWEST capacity (0.286); D96 (L = 0.531) has the HIGHEST (0.990).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_048"]),
                    new("The spectral gap predicts adaptability, inversely", "ρ(capacity, λ₂) = −0.841 versus ρ(capacity, L) = −0.029 — a factor ≈29, with the sign INVERTED: a SMALLER gap ⇒ a MORE adaptable graph (D_048). Near-degeneracy, not degeneracy count, indexes adaptability: D96's gap (0.3864) is 248× smaller than K96's (96) and its capacity 3.5× larger. The claim 'L predicts adaptability better than λ₂' is REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_048"]),
                    new("Degeneracy-free graphs cannot adapt", "An all-singleton spectrum has nothing to unsplit: the sparse random graph keeps L = 0 and capacity = 0 EXACTLY, with ΔA ≡ ΔE ≡ 0 at every dose and every perturbation type (D_048). Every degenerate case adapts (capacity > 0.25); random does not adapt at all — yet it is nearly the most stable graph (recovery 0.9792), so recovery and adaptability trade off.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_048"]),
                    new("Weight perturbation is the strongest lever", "Under equal relative damage, perturbing every edge weight is the universally strongest perturbation (capacity 1.000 on every structured graph), while exact zeros mark structurally impossible perturbations: adding an edge is undefined on K96 (no non-edge) and inert on the unphysical profile, and rewiring K96 is a no-op because its only non-edge is the edge just removed (D_048).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_048"]),
                    new("Adaptability × Robustness is not constant", "There is NO universal tradeoff Adaptability × Robustness = const (D_049). The product spans 0 → 0.956 (the degenerate-free null gives exactly 0) and 0.282 → 0.956 among the five non-null cases — span 3.39×, CV 0.326. The conserved-product law has the WRONG SIGN: capacity RISES with damage, C = 7.98·Δ^(+0.737) with R² = 0.720, whereas C·Δ = const demands exponent −1; forcing −1 gives R² = −3.282, worse than predicting the mean. A doubling of damage buys only 1.666× capacity.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_049"]),
                    new("The frontier has exactly two members", "The (capacity, recovery) Pareto frontier of the six cases has EXACTLY TWO members (D_049): D96 (max adaptability, 0.9902) and complete (max robustness, 0.9868). D96³, random, physical and unphysical are STRICTLY DOMINATED — they give up adaptability without buying any robustness. So a tradeoff EXISTS, but it is a 2-point frontier, not a conserved quantity.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_049"]),
                    new("One quantity, two signs", "A single structural quantity governs both axes of the frontier with OPPOSITE signs (D_049): the degeneracy COUNT gives ρ(capacity) = +0.886 and ρ(recovery) = −0.543; equivalently the inverse spectral gap gives ρ(capacity, λ₂) = −0.841 and ρ(recovery, λ₂) = +0.464 (ρ(degeneracy, λ₂) = −0.814). The degeneracy FRACTION L is a far weaker predictor (ρ(capacity, L) = −0.029) — the COUNT indexes the tradeoff, not the share, sharpening D_048.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_049"]),
                    new("The frontier is not universal", "Frontier membership depends on the perturbation family (D_049). Under edge DELETION the frontier collapses to a SINGLE case ({physical} is best on both axes — no tradeoff at all); edge addition gives four members {D96, physical, unphysical, complete}; rewiring three {D96, physical, complete}; weight three {D96, random, complete}. No single family reproduces the aggregate frontier. Universality therefore fails twice: no conserved constant, and no family-invariant frontier. The fitted constants (β ≈ 0.74, k ≈ 7.98) are EMERGENT ensemble numbers with no derivation.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-D_049"]),
                    new("One spectral quantity, not four", "Adaptability and robustness can be predicted from spectral quantities only as an ORDERING (D_050). The four candidate inputs are NOT independent facts: λ₂ ↔ near-gap density ρ = 0.986, degeneracy ↔ near-gap −0.886, λ₂ ↔ degeneracy −0.841, and the distinct-eigenvalue count correlates with nothing (|ρ| ≤ 0.37, spanning only 94 levels). Parsimony by leave-one-out selects ONE predictor for BOTH targets — the near-gap density: capacity = −0.00814·(near-gap) + 0.87153 (R² = 0.759) and recovery = +0.000284·(near-gap) + 0.95582 (R² = 0.525) — one axis read with opposite signs, exactly D_049's mechanism.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_050"]),
                    new("A four-input spectral law overfits", "The all-four-input model for capacity has the BEST in-sample R² (0.972) and the WORST out-of-sample error (D_050): leave-one-out RMSE 31.0123, i.e. 105× the single-predictor 0.2962, on five parameters over six cases. Its two-input variant is worse than its own singleton (0.3164 vs 0.2962). REFUTED: 'a four-input spectral law determines capacity and recovery', and 'λ₂, near-gap density and the distinct count are independent predictors'.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_050"]),
                    new("The spectrum orders, it does not determine", "Residuals of the minimal capacity fit reach 0.2939 — 30 % of the FULL capacity range — and every residual above 0.10 sits at an endpoint of the degeneracy axis (D_050: D96 −0.1349, random +0.2939, complete −0.1872). What does the real work is DERIVED, not fitted: 0 ≤ capacity ≤ 1 (ΔA ≤ N − A₀ headroom, D_048), 0 ≤ recovery ≤ 1, and the exact zero-degeneracy null. Together with D_049's family collapse, the honest form is 'capacity and recovery are ORDERED by the degeneracy axis', NOT 'determined by the spectrum'.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_050"]),
                    new("The prediction fails out of sample", "Run prospectively on six new 96-node rings, the D_050 one-predictor law misses badly (D_051). Capacity mean absolute error 0.1305 against an OBSERVED span of only 0.0631 — the error is 2.07× the entire range of the truth — and the recovery prediction captures just 3.1 % of the real variation. Rank error for capacity is 2.00 of 5 with Spearman ρ = −0.131 (anti-correlated). Every miss is an under-prediction.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_051"]),
                    new("The predictor is constant on rings", "Near-gap = 2 is FORCED on every 1-D ring whose low-k spectrum follows the k² law (D_051): λ₂/λ₁ = 4 and λ₃/λ₂ = 9/4 > 2, so exactly the k = ±1 doublet lies within 2λ₂ — two modes, whatever the weight profile. Five of the six new rings therefore receive one identical prediction (capacity 0.8553) against six distinct measurements. D_050's minimal predictor set is a CONSTANT on the ring family, the family D96 belongs to, so its R² = 0.759 must have been carried by the non-ring cases (random, complete, the 3-D torus).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_051"]),
                    new("Rings adapt regardless of profile", "Six structurally different 96-node rings all adapt strongly (D_051): capacity 0.9369 … 1.0000 for sparse (±1..±3), dense (±1..±12), tapered (w = 1/d), long-range-weighted (w = d) and extended (±24, ±48) rings alike — each collects nearly all of its headroom. The measured recovery band (0.9198 … 0.9754) is 33× wider than the one-predictor law predicted. The degeneracy count varied across the same rings (38 … 47) while the near-gap density did not, making it the concrete next candidate.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_051"]),
                    new("The degeneracy count predicts ring capacity", "Within the seven-ring family the degeneracy count is the better capacity predictor (D_052): Spearman ρ = +0.815 versus the near-gap density's +0.204 (4×), refit R² 0.226 vs 0.068, and frozen-coefficient mean |error| 0.07399 vs 0.13116 (1.8×). The sign is POSITIVE, matching D_050's cross-ensemble +0.886 — so 'more degenerate levels ⇒ more adaptable' now holds WITHIN one topological family, not only across unrelated graphs. All seven rings adapt strongly (0.93693 … 1.00000) despite a 4× degree and 90× λ₂ spread.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_052"]),
                    new("The degeneracy axis is not injective", "The degeneracy axis cannot be a sufficient predictor, and this is a proof rather than a measurement (D_052): D96, S96-123 and Ring48 share the IDENTICAL multiplicity spectrum 1×6, 1×5, 42×2, 1×1 — hence identical degeneracy count (44), entropy (3.7620) and ΔE_lock (0.8023) — so every functional of the multiplicity spectrum assigns them one number by construction, while their λ₂ values differ by a factor of 90. Their MEASURED capacities span 0.02320 = 37 % of the whole family range (recovery 22 %). The axis is a coarse invariant: better than near-gap (5 groups vs 2), not sufficient.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_052"]),
                    new("Recovery goes the other way", "For recovery the near-gap density remains the better orderer within rings (D_052): ρ = +0.408 versus the degeneracy count's −0.222. The degeneracy axis does NOT replace the D_050 predictor — it replaces it for capacity only. LOO stability favours near-gap for both targets, and the extra degeneracy inputs are worse still: adding the multiplicity entropy or D_047's ΔE_lock (which are exactly anti-correlated here, with byte-identical rankings) multiplies the frozen-coefficient error by 5 (0.38469 vs 0.07399) — a second instance of D_050's overfitting lesson.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_052"]),
                    new("Spectrum controls capacity, not the family", "Within the seven-ring family the RING (spectrum) factor owns 74.5 % of capacity's cell variance against the perturbation family's 15.7 %, with 9.8 % interaction (D_053, two-way layout over 7 rings × 4 families). The family spread inside a bit-for-bit fixed spectrum is 0.39× the spectrum spread at fixed family. The control is asymmetric and the conclusion is therefore conservative: a family change perturbs the SAME adjacency (λ₂, degeneracy count, entropy and ΔE_lock all exactly fixed), so the family axis is the perfectly controlled one — and still the smaller effect.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_053"]),
                    new("The family effect is real but second", "The perturbation family is a real, consistently ordered control (D_053): across the six rings with any variation, weight and rewire are always the top two and delete and add always the bottom two — a 2+2 split — while the order within each pair flips ring to ring, which is exactly the 9.8 % interaction. Family main effects on capacity: weight 0.9891 > rewire 0.9886 > add 0.9706 > delete 0.9695. Weight perturbation breaks every symmetry at once without removing a single edge, reproducing D_048's side finding on a new family.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_053"]),
                    new("Capacity saturates, so the question has a regime", "Capacity is a SATURATING quantity, and §4's fixed-ABSOLUTE-dose check shows the spectrum effect is not a pure ring property (D_053): the delete-family spectrum spread falls 0.0771 (fractional doses) → 0.0351 (k = 13 edges) → 0.0065 (k = 29) → 0.0000 (k = 58), and at k = 58 the FAMILY spread is also exactly 0.0000 — every sampled cell at capacity 1.0000. Decay96 is exactly family-invariant (1.0000 in all four families) because its spectrum 47×2, 2×1 has no multiplicity greater than 2. So 'family versus spectrum' is ill-posed outside the unsaturated regime, which is small: 58 edges is 5 % of a degree-24 ring and 20 % of a degree-6 ring.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_053"]),
                    new("Family governs frontier membership, not size", "This refines D_049: its family finding — edge deletion collapsing the adaptability–robustness frontier to a single case — was measured across a HETEROGENEOUS case set (D_096, the 3-D torus, random, complete, physical, unphysical). Within one homogeneous topological family the family effect, though real and exactly measurable, is not dominant (D_053: 15.7 % vs 74.5 % for capacity). Family membership therefore governs WHICH cases a frontier contains, not how large a case's response is — while the family axis remains the better-behaved control, since it needs no dose matching and keeps a signature where capacity has saturated.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_053", "ResearchY-D_049"]),
                    new("A ring's edge shape is one number", "On a circulant ring the eigenvalue at wavevector k equals the one at N − k, so the spectrum bottom is a DOUBLET: λ₂ = λ₃ and λ₄ = λ₅ in 12 of the 13 rings audited (D_054). Hence r3 = λ₃/λ₂ is EXACTLY 1 — two distinct values in the whole family — and r5 ≡ r4. Only ONE scale-free number survives: r4 = λ₄/λ₂, the second folded level over the first, which is 4.000000 for a pure parabolic edge λ_k ∝ k² and therefore measures EDGE BENDING: 3.995718 (E1) → 3.894202 (D96) → 3.617858 (D96-24) → 1.018556 (Ring48). Declaring λ₃, λ₄, λ₅ adds no information: all four scale inputs give identical ρ to three decimals on both targets.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_054"]),
                    new("The edge shape does not control capacity", "REFUTED: the best edge-shape predictor of ring capacity reaches Spearman ρ = 0.214 (exact permutation p = 0.6615) against the degeneracy count's 0.815 (p = 0.0310) — a factor of four (D_054). The shape ratios are NEGATIVELY correlated: ρ(r4) = ρ(r5) = −0.429. On the held-out six-ring set the edge inputs are the worse predictors still (ρ = 0.143, exact p = 0.8028), even though they are the ONLY predictors that resolve all six rings there.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_054"]),
                    new("Resolution is not predictiveness", "The sharpest result of D_054. On the held-out six edge-shape rings both D_052 predictors take only TWO distinct values — near-gap 2,2,13,2,2,2 and degeneracy 47,47,47,47,47,23 — separating exactly one ring each, while λ₂ … λ₅ and r4, r5 take SIX, the only inputs that distinguish every ring. The edge inputs nonetheless LOSE: capacity ρ = 0.143 against the degeneracy count's 0.655, and LOO 0.35984 against 0.23353. An input can resolve a whole family and still carry no usable information about which member adapts.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_054"]),
                    new("The bounds disqualify the shape ratios", "0 ≤ capacity, recovery ≤ 1 are identities (D_050), so a predictor that leaves the range is wrong by construction. Of 182 cells in D_054, the scale inputs λ₂ … λ₅ never leave [0,1] — the source set spans λ₂ from 0.386 to 96, a far wider envelope than any ring — while the ratios leave it 12 times (r4 and r5 six each, worst E1 capacity 1.09533) and r3 once (−0.49307 on G1248). Admissibility and resolution pull in opposite directions: the input admissible in linear form does not resolve rings, and the descriptor that resolves rings is inadmissible in linear form.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_054"]),
                    new("Pair1-47 breaks the ring family", "The near-antipodal ring ±1 and ±47 is the only audited ring whose spectrum bottom is NOT the folded doublet pair — its λ₂ sits at k = 2 rather than k = 1 (near-gap 13, degeneracy count 23, 25 distinct levels) — and it is the only one whose capacity falls to 0.42089, against 0.93693 … 1.00000 for every other ring on either case set (D_054). An existence statement about the ring family, not a predictor: the family is not uniform, and the outlier is identified by its geometry rather than by any numeric fit. Its headroom (96 − 25 = 71) is the largest in the family, yet only about 42 % of it is collected.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_054"]),
                    new("One level holds half the spectrum", "Pair1-47's anomaly is a SINGLE DEGENERATE LEVEL HOLDING 52.1 % OF THE SPECTRUM — multiplicity 50 of 96 (D_055). No healthy ring's largest level holds more than 12 of 96 (12.5 %); the median holds 6. The exact cause is arithmetic: the offset 47 = N/2 − 1 satisfies cos(2πk·47/96) = (−1)^k·cos(2πk/96), so the two offsets' contributions are exactly complementary for every ODD k, giving λ_k = 4 exactly for all 48 odd k (plus k = 24, 72). Verified: 48 odd modes, largest |λ_k − 4| = 0, and 50 eigenvalues within 1e-9 of 4 in the actual Laplacian spectrum. In general {±1, ±d} gives this level exactly when d ≡ ±1 (mod N/2).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("The rank budget bounds adaptability", "Deleting or adding an edge subtracts a RANK-2 matrix, and a rank-r perturbation acts on an m-fold eigenspace as an m×m matrix of rank ≤ r, which has at most r + 1 distinct eigenvalues — so the achievable attractor increase is bounded LEVEL BY LEVEL (D_055): ΔA ≤ Σ_i min(m_i − 1, r), r ≤ 2k. A spectrum whose headroom sits in one huge level is a RANK TRAP; the healthy rings are rank-efficient because their headroom is many small levels that one edge operation releases together. Measured (delete family, ceiling vs measured): Pair1-47 k=1 0.3380/0.3239 → k=19 0.8451/0.5728; D96 k=1 0.9020/0.8562 → k=3 1.0000/0.9281. Capacity depends on the multiplicity DISTRIBUTION, not its count or the headroom.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("Localization cannot discriminate", "Eigenvector localization and the participation ratio are REJECTED as explanations of any ring anomaly, in principle rather than by measurement (D_055): every audited ring is a circulant, so every Laplacian eigenvector is a Fourier mode with |v_i| = 1/√N and PR = EXACTLY N = 96 for every mode of every ring — verified with the exact Fourier modes as eigenvectors. Likewise all these rings share the same symmetry class (the dihedral group of order 2N, one orbit, vertex-transitive). Neither observable can separate any two members of the family.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("Many degenerate levels do not collapse capacity", "The degeneracy COUNT is ANTI-correlated with Pair1-47's collapse (D_055): it has the FEWEST degenerate groups in the family (23, against D96's 44) and one enormous level. λ₂, mean level spacing, degree and edge count also fail to separate it — E1 has a 4.85× smaller λ₂ (0.004282 vs 0.034221) and half the degree while being perfectly healthy at capacity 0.98598. The separators that do work (ΔE_lock, headroom, A₀, largest multiplicity and its share) are all restatements of one multiplicity multiset, which is why the degeneracy count of D_052 and the near-gap density of D_050 cannot see the anomaly.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("The perturbator's coin is not random", "A defect in the shared perturbation ensemble, found by D_055: the weight family reads its sign from the LOWEST BIT of the LCG x ← 1664525x + 1013904223 (mod 2³²). Both coefficients are odd, so (a·x + c) mod 2 = (x + 1) mod 2 and the coin ALTERNATES deterministically for every seed (reproduced: 0 1 0 1 …, 1 0 1 0 …). Measured consequences: the weight family yields exactly TWO distinct weight values (Pair1-47 0.98×96 and 1.02×96; D96 0.98×288 and 1.02×288); the reweighted graph is neither circulant nor shift-symmetric, so it is a fixed pattern rather than a preserved symmetry; and the effect is spectrum-dependent — D96 still reaches 96 distinct eigenvalues while Pair1-47 stalls at 51 with its λ = 4 level intact. With a corrected coin (high bits, independent sign per edge) Pair1-47's weight capacity rises 0.3662 → 0.9812. Scope: the weight family only — delete, add and rewire draw from the full 32-bit value and stand, so the direction of every audit since D_048 (weight strongest) is unaffected.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("The multiplicity distribution predicts capacity", "Capacity is strongly predicted by the multiplicity distribution — but its five standard statistics are ONE axis, not five (D_056). Max multiplicity, Gini(m) and the largest-level share are mutually monotone with IDENTICAL ρ (−0.964 on capacity), and Entropy(m) (+0.964) and Herfindahl(m) (−0.964) are the exact mirror. On the seven-ring case set the axis gives ρ = 0.964 with exact permutation p = 0.0024 and R² = 0.995 — the first result in the D group to SURVIVE multiple-comparison correction (α/5 = 0.01). Removing Pair1-47 moves ρ only to 0.941, and on the six healthy rings the distribution's R² (up to 0.784) beats the degeneracy count's 0.528. Its sign is NEGATIVE: the more concentrated the spectrum, the less capacity.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_056"]),
                    new("The distribution adds no ordering", "The full multiplicity distribution does NOT beat the degeneracy count on ordering (D_056): on the required case set both give ρ = 0.964 with the IDENTICAL exact p = 0.0024, so the five statistics carry no rank information the count does not already have. It wins only on value fidelity — R² 0.995 against 0.987, and 0.784 against 0.528 on the healthy six. And it is not injective: D96, S96-123 and Ring48 share one distribution exactly, so every functional assigns them one number while their capacities spread 0.02320. The near-gap baseline is worse off still — six of seven rings share one value, so its LOO is UNDEFINED, not merely poor.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_056"]),
                    new("The distribution says nothing about recovery", "Every multiplicity-distribution statistic gives ρ = 0.037 on recovery with exact permutation p = 0.9786 — indistinguishable from no association (D_056) — while λ₂ reaches ρ = 0.821 there. The distribution controls adaptability and not robustness, a clean separation of the two axes D_049 identified.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_056"]),
                    new("A ring collapse was predicted in advance", "D_056's blind test confirmed a sharp structural forecast exactly. P47-48 — built by adding the exactly-antipodal ±48 offset to Pair1-47 — was predicted to KEEP its dominant level, moved from λ = 4 to λ = 8, because the ±48 term contributes 2(1 − cos πk) = 4 to every odd mode. Measured: the dominant level sits at λ = 8.000000 with multiplicity 49, and the ring's capacity collapses to 0.58920 — the only collapsing ring of the four new ones (P47-16 0.69765, P47-123 0.99618, H51123 0.97455). Its λ₂ is IDENTICAL to Pair1-47's (0.034221) and its near-gap is 2, so the near-gap predictor cannot see it at all; all five distribution statistics and D_055's rank ceiling order the four new rings perfectly (ρ = ±1.000).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_056"]),
                    new("The rank budget is the best predictor", "D_055's derived rank-budget ceiling ΔA ≤ Σ min(mᵢ − 1, r), the only input derived from the multiplicity distribution by ARGUMENT rather than fitted from it, leads on every fidelity measure (D_056): R² = 0.978 on the six healthy rings against the degeneracy count's 0.528, LOO 0.00511 there, R² = 0.999 and ρ = 1.000 on the four blind rings, and the smallest frozen-coefficient error of any input. It also explains why fitting coefficients on the non-ring source cases extrapolates badly onto rings — every fitted input has a frozen mean error of 0.19 … 0.49.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_056", "ResearchY-D_055"]),
                    new("The rank bound is a derived law", "The bound ΔA ≤ Σ min(mᵢ − 1, r) is verified as a LAW, not a correlation (D_057). Its lemma holds absolutely: 3200 random symmetric rank-r perturbations on degenerate eigenspaces (m ∈ {4, 8, 16}, r = 1…6) split into EXACTLY r + 1 parts at the maximum with ZERO violations, because a rank-r perturbation acts on an m-fold eigenspace as a rank-≤ r matrix. On the real ensemble the bound holds in 600 of 600 samples. And a ZERO-PARAMETER version — no fitting of any kind — orders the rings at ρ = 0.873 and lands within 0.0143 of the measurement on the seven spread rings, including one exact zero-error case (Decay96).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_057"]),
                    new("Tightness is governed by concentration", "How much of the ceiling is actually collected is ordered by how concentrated the spectrum is (D_057): tightness runs 0.9653 … 1.0000 for spread multiplicity structures but 0.6633 … 0.8945 for dominant-level ones, with ρ(largest-level share, tightness) = −0.969. The reason is the rank budget — tens of small levels split simultaneously, while one huge level consumes all of r in a single eigenspace. So the law's accuracy is itself a function of the multiplicity distribution, which explains D_056: the distribution was predicting the TIGHTNESS, not the bound.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_057"]),
                    new("The law is not closed by structure and rank", "REFUTED as complete (D_057): the bound is an UPPER bound that is nearly achieved by spread structures (mean error 0.0143) and missed by a factor of 10 by concentrated ones (0.1431) — it bounds capacity, it does not equal it. Rank alone does not close the gap either: at the 0.5 % dose two rings with IDENTICAL multiplicity patterns and headroom get the same k = 1, r = 2 and the same ceiling 0.3380, yet measure 0.23005 and 0.32394. And a single pooled tightness correction (L3) is the worst of the three law forms (mean error 0.21259). The residual beyond (structure, rank) points at the geometry of the perturbation's projection onto the degenerate eigenspace.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_057"]),
                    new("Two ring collapses called in advance", "D_057's blind design called both verdicts correctly, from one offset's difference. Half47 (±1, ±24, ±47) was predicted to KEEP its dominant level at a third value — the ±24 term contributes 2(1 − cos(πk/2)) = 2 for odd k, so the odd-mode sum is 2 + (2 − 2cos θ) + (2 + 2cos θ) = 6 — and the measurement found λ = 6.000000 with multiplicity 50 and capacity 0.61479. Triple47 (±1, ±23, ±47) was predicted to LOSE the level because 23 = N/4 − 1 gives 6 ∓ 2 sin θ with no cancellation — measured max multiplicity 2, capacity 0.99823, fully healthy. The law tracks the LEVEL, not any particular offset.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_057"]),
                    new("Recovery is a magnitude, capacity a count", "Why did five audits fail to predict recovery while predicting capacity? Because the two are different OBSERVABLE CLASSES (D_058). Capacity is a COUNT — ΔA = A₁ − A₀, the number of new distinct eigenvalues, which the rank budget bounds exactly. Recovery is a MAGNITUDE — 1 − ‖λ′−λ‖₂/‖λ‖₂, a relative spectral displacement — and a bound on how MANY new levels appear says nothing about HOW FAR they move. Measured: ρ(ΔA, relative shift) = 0.046 over 480 samples, near-orthogonal; and the IDENTICAL rank ceiling gives ρ = 0.873 on capacity but only 0.409 on recovery. So recovery is not a failed prediction — it is outside the count predictors' type.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_058"]),
                    new("The magnitude law", "Recovery has its own derived law, the counterpart of the rank bound (D_058): 1 − recovery ≈ c·(‖δA‖_F/‖λ‖₂), from first-order perturbation theory δλ_i = vᵢᵀ δA vᵢ. With ONE coefficient calibrated on the source cases it reaches ρ = 0.905 and mean |error| 0.00767 out-of-sample — the best of everything tried. The denominator is the content: ‖δA‖_F alone gives ρ = −0.738. And the spectral norm is EXACT, not fitted — for a d-regular unweighted ring trace L² = dN(d+1), so ‖λ‖₂ = √(N·d·(d+1)) identically, confirmed as 122.38 = 122.38 (d=12), 43.82 = 43.82 (d=4), 63.50 = 63.50 (d=6) and 240.00 = 240.00 (d=24).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_058"]),
                    new("Scale tells the two mechanisms apart", "Three frozen predictions, all confirmed, on two rings with EXACTLY D96's offsets at weight 4× and 1/4× (D_058). Capacity is IDENTICAL across all three (spread 0.00033) — the count mechanism is blind to a global scale. Recovery under the weight family is scale-invariant with spread EXACTLY 0.00000. And recovery under INSERTION is scale-sensitive (add family: 0.99033 → 0.95655 → 0.75829) while deletion is also exactly invariant — because a proportional removal leaves ‖δA‖_F/‖λ‖₂ unchanged while a fixed-unit-weight insertion does not. That asymmetry was sharper than the frozen prediction anticipated.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-D_058"]),
                    new("Recovery's band is narrow, so its metrics flatter", "The honest caveat of D_058, stated rather than buried: recovery spans only 0.0556 across the audited rings against capacity's 0.5791 — a band 10.4× narrower. Every ρ for recovery is therefore inflated and every error deflated, and the magnitude law's mean |error| of 0.0077 is 14 % of the entire recovery range, not 1 %. The correct reading is that the law captures the ORDERING and explains the scale sensitivity, not that recovery is predicted to within a percent. Also refuted: any multiplicity-distribution grip on recovery (ρ = 0.024), and the perturbation family as a ring-level determinant (ρ = 0.000 as a per-ring average — its effect lives within a ring).", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-D_058"]),
                    new("Measurement is the top V2.2 program", "MEASUREMENT ORIGIN ranks highest (19/20) among ten V2.2 candidates (NP_002): it uses the derived chain most directly — Difference (D_039), magnitude, phase, complex states (D_036), the reciprocal two-quadrature measurement basis (D_037), and Actualization. Completing the QM chain (QG73 collapse + QG74 measurement + D_037 + D_039) is the recommended first project.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_002"]),
                    new("Measurement event derived", "A measurement event is an ACTUALIZATION EVENT on a DISTINGUISHABLE state (M_001): it reads both quadratures of one complex mode (the {cos, sin} basis, D_037), actualizing the state's identity with Born weight (QG216). State identity/observability/probability DERIVED; the measurement event and its collapse reading EMERGENT — QG73 resolved as the event's binary reading.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_001"]),
                    new("Measurement disturbs minimally", "The minimal unavoidable disturbance of a measurement is PHASE-PINNING (M_002): reading both quadratures extracts and fixes the phase; magnitude/identity/probability survive. Measurement without disturbance is impossible; repeated measurements are idempotent; the complex state is basis-invariant. Disturbance DERIVED from the read.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_002"]),
                    new("Measurement feeds forward", "A measurement outcome necessarily changes future evolution (M_003): the pinned phase becomes the initial condition of the deterministic trajectory, θ_t = θ₀ + t·Δθ (Δθ = 2πk/N, D_041). Measured future fixed, unmeasured a superposition; future interference needs the outcome fed back. Feedback DERIVED; evolution DERIVED (D_041).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_003"]),
                    new("Measurement information limit", "The maximum information content of one measurement event is log₂(95) ≈ 6.57 bits (M_004) — the size of the distinguishable state space (D_039). A measurement resolves which of 95 states is realized (gain log₂ 95); repeated measurements are idempotent (0 additional info); measurement creates information. Information DERIVED; event EMERGENT (M_001).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_004"]),
                    new("Information conserved in measurement", "Measurement REVEALS pre-existing distinguishability and REDISTRIBUTES it — it does NOT create information (M_005). The 6.57 bits pre-exist in the state space (D_039); log₂ 95 = outcome + observer (conserved). Underlying: count conservation (Born rule Σ|ψ|²=1, QG216).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_005"]),
                    new("Observer is the epistemic recipient", "The observer's role (M_006) is the RECIPIENT of the information redistribution — it changes only epistemic access, not the ontic state. The state (D_039), observability, and the reconstruction map z=a+ib (D_037) are observer-independent (DERIVED); the observer role and epistemic access are EMERGENT. Measurement program complete (M_001–M_006).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_006"]),
                    new("Measurement chain classified", "The chain D96 → pairing → complex state → reciprocity → observability → measurement is fully classified (M_007): pairing/complex state/observability DERIVED; reciprocity/measurement/observer EMERGENT; disturbance/feedback/information/conservation DERIVED. Only the five R_001 boundaries remain.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_007"]),
                    new("Two measurement predictions", "The measurement chain is mostly QM-equivalent (idempotence, basis, complementarity, Born), with two candidate AT-specific predictions (M_008): AT-P042 — post-measurement phase advances per actualization tick (Δθ=2πk/N, discrete time); AT-P043 — one event reveals at most log₂ 95 ≈ 6.57 bits.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_008"]),
                    new("Discriminator: one prediction survives", "The M_009 discriminator keeps exactly one uniquely-AT prediction. AT-P042 is C — genuinely new: the discrete tick time-parameter gives a FINITE phase lattice {θ₀+m·2πk/N} of cardinality N/gcd(N,k) ≤ 96 vs QM's phase continuum (step DERIVED, D_041). AT-P043 is A — already implied: log₂(d) per event is the standard d-outcome Shannon bound (QM imposes the same); only the value d=95 is AT-derived (D_039). AT-P042 remains PREDICTION; AT-P043 downgraded to CORRESPONDENCE.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_009"]),
                    new("Phase lattice is observably QM-equivalent", "M_010: AT-P042's discrete phase evolution produces NO observable effect beyond continuous QM at any tick-sampled time. Continuous QM with ω=2πk/(N·τ) reproduces phase, recurrence (N/gcd(N,k)), interference (2π(k₁−k₂)/N per tick), and finite-state orbits exactly at ticks. Only the sub-tick phase differs (in-principle-only — the tick is the fundamental clock). Tick observables CORRESPONDENCE; discrete time-parameter structural PREDICTION; nothing falsified.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_010"]),
                    new("No exact O(3): the ring is dihedral", "M_011: no exact effective O(3) emerges from D96 actualization dynamics. Aut(C96(±1..±6)) = D₉₆ (order 192, irreps 4×1D + 47×2D — no dimension ≥ 3); the only multiplier stabilizing S={±1..±6} is a=±1. The spectrum (95 modes, 44 distinct eigenvalues) has multiplicities {2×42, 5, 6} — mirror doublets plus octave blocks — NOT a 2l+1 ladder; spherical-harmonic degeneracies intersect it only at {5}. Shell closures [4,8,95] do not reproduce magic numbers; π (transcendental) never appears in the algebraic spectrum (role EMERGENT, value BOUNDARY, B_002); S∝A structure EMERGENT but the exact 1/4 is REFUTED (needs imported 2π, QG196); nuclear closures REFUTED (confirms NP_087/089). Leading-order large-scale isotropy EMERGENT only (NP_089).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_011"]),
                    new("Network yields one genuine 3D sector: the p-wave", "M_012: a NETWORK of three coupled D96 rings on orthogonal axes (D96⊗D96⊗D96, cubic lattice) has axis point group O_h (order 48; irreps {1,1,2,3,3,1,1,2,3,3}) — unlike the single ring (Aut = D₉₆, max irrep dim 2). The defining vector rep on ℝ³ is irreducible (⟨χ,χ⟩=1), so a genuine 3D sector EMERGES: the p-wave triplet (l=1, degeneracy 3 = 2l+1, subduction l=1→T₁u unsplit). Spherical subduction keeps only l=0 (1) and l=1 (3) full; l=2 → 2+3, l=3 → 1+3+3 split (character norms² 1,1,2,3,4). Joint spectrum (λ_a+λ_b+λ_c): 16,080 distinct levels; first-excited degeneracy 6 = {±x̂,±ŷ,±ẑ} (O_h orbit, not a 2l+1 shell). Magic numbers, full 2l+1 ladder, π value, and exact Bekenstein 1/4 remain REFUTED; π role EMERGENT. M_011's single-ring NO confirmed.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_012"]),
                    new("Three axes is hosted; three is the self-dual count", "M_013: why exactly 3 coupled D96 axes? The count 3 is NOT dynamically forced — point groups B_d = 2^d·d! (2, 8, 48, 384) have no distinguished member; information d·log₂95 is additive with no extremum; family count 3 is a single-ring span-window [4,8) quantity decoupled from axes (NP_037); DOS p = d is an identity (only d ≥ 3 load-bearing via (d−2), QG197). The exact 3 is merely sufficient as the HOSTED spatial dimension (CORRESPONDENCE). It is UNIQUE in one derived sense: rotation self-duality d(d−1)/2 = d ⇒ d = 3, so(3) ≅ ℝ³ — only three axes make rotations a vector of the same dimension as space. D₄ (2 axes) has no genuine 3D irrep; O_h (3 axes) does; defining rep of B_d is irreducible of dimension d. M_012's genuine 3D p/vector sector is EMERGENT at the self-dual d = 3.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_013"]),
                    new("N=96 wins an exhaustive, non-manual scan", "M_014: score EVERY ring N = 16..512 (497 rings, no manual candidate selection) with Score(N) = [0 unpaired] + [3 families ∧ span < 8] + [6|N] + [N = 3·2^k]. Distribution {4:1, 3:14, 2:101, 1:255, 0:126}; N = 96 is the UNIQUE Score-4 maximizer (A B C D = 1 1 1 1; 3 families; 44 eigenvalues; pattern {2×42,5×1,6×1}; span 6.403 < 8). Score-3 = seed-3 rungs at wrong family count (24, 48, 192, 384) + D_029 zero-defect rings {60..120}\\{96}. Drop analysis: removing the 3-family window admits the 5 rungs; removing the octave rung admits the 11 zero-defect rings; removing pairing or 6-divisibility leaves 96 unique. Confirms D_029/D_030/D_031 without any hand-picked candidate windows or ladders.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_014"]),
                    new("The M_014 ranking survives perturbation of the score", "M_015: for each criterion of the M_014 Score(N) remove it, value-perturb it, and threshold-perturb it (±5/±10/±20%), recomputing the FULL rank table over all 497 rings per variant (26 variants, no manual selection). Verdict ROBUST: N = 96 is in the argmax at rank 1 in 24/26 variants (every removal — remove A/C keeps {96} unique, remove B admits the 5 rungs, remove D the 11 zero-defect rings — and every value/threshold perturbation that keeps the physical family-3 requirement); the only two displacements are the family-req → 2/4 re-targets, which change criterion B into a different physical requirement and select the family-2 rung 48 / family-4 rung 192. Unique top {96} in 16/26 and in ALL ±5/±10% threshold variants (U ∈ [7.2,8.8], pairing ≤ 2, divisors 4/8/12, rung tol ≤ 0.08 oct). Score(96) ∈ {4,3} (4 in 17, 3 in 9 = removals + razor drops); never below 3, never beaten. Razor edges (uniqueness only): U must stay > span(96) = 6.4025 (the exact −19.97% razor; −20% → 6.40 → 11-ring tie, 96 still in it); rung tolerance ≥ 0.10 oct admits {90, 96, 102}; divisor must divide 96.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_015"]),
                    new("The four M_014 criteria are partially redundant", "M_016: over N = 16..512 (497 rings) the criteria A = 0 unpaired, B = 3 families ∧ span<8, C = 6|N, D = N = 3·2^k hold on 354/61/83/5 rings. They are NOT independent: a deterministic inclusion chain D ⊆ C ⊆ A holds (every seed-3 rung is 6-divisible and zero-unpaired; every 6-divisible ring in range is zero-unpaired), joint H = 2.017 bits vs ΣH = 2.135 → total correlation 0.1179 bits = 5.5%; pairwise phi up to 0.285 (A–C). Yet no criterion is a function of the others: unique-information fractions H(X_i|others)/H(X_i) = 0.895/0.999/0.827/0.672 (A/B/C/D), B is statistically near-independent (I(B;·) ≤ 0.0004 bit), and PCA of the standardized criteria has eigenvalues [1.397, 1.001, 0.935, 0.667] (participation ratio 3.744 ≈ 4 effective dimensions). WHICH CRITERION SELECTS N = 96: the pair {B, D} — B holds exactly on the 3-family window [60,120] (61 rings), D on the rung ladder {24,48,96,192,384} (5 rings), and B∧D = {96}; A and C are implied by D and add no further selection. Verdict PARTIALLY REDUNDANT, confirming from information theory that B and D are the load-bearing discriminators (M_014 drop analysis, M_015).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-M_016"]),
                    new("The phase is the one lever", "NP_003: the theory has exactly ONE controllable lever — the phase θ₀ of a complex state. Locally variable (B): a measurement pins it (M_002) and it becomes the future initial condition (θ_t = θ₀ + t·Δθ, M_003). Changes time behaviour + measurement; NOT frequency, gravity, or sector structure. Everything else (Difference, η, N=96, spectrum, ω₁, λ₂, anchors {v,m_e}) is fixed; no global lever exists.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_003"]),
                    new("Phase couples, but does not synchronize", "NP_004: the phase is a TRUE lever — it couples through interference (I = ρ_A+ρ_B+2√(ρ_Aρ_B)·cos(θ_A−θ_B)) and through a shared actualization event (joint pinning, M_002). But synchronization requires IDENTICAL modes: θ_A−θ_B = (θ_A0−θ_B0) + t(Δθ_A−Δθ_B) is time-invariant only if k_A = k_B; unequal modes drift (no locking force). Sustained phase relations are common-origin correlations.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_004"]),
                    new("Missing synchronization mechanism", "NP_005: unequal-mode phase locking requires a cross-phase feedback term κ·sin(θ_B−θ_A) absent from the canonical chain (the update θ(t+1)=θ(t)+Δθ has only the self-rate). The term gives dψ/dt = Δθ_A−Δθ_B−2κ·sin(ψ), a stable fixed point iff κ ≥ |Δθ_A−Δθ_B|/2 (0.5236 for k=16,32). Equal modes synchronize trivially; the locking force is BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_005"]),
                    new("Locking term is Born-derived in form", "NP_006: the locking term's form is the interference gradient — ∂I/∂θ_A = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B)·sin(θ_B−θ_A) (I = ρ_A+ρ_B+2√(ρ_Aρ_B)·cos(θ_A−θ_B), Born QG216) — with coefficient κ = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B), a DERIVED cross-amplitude, not free. But the MECHANISM (a gradient-following phase update) is absent in canonical AT — it would be EMERGENT only under a variational principle.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_006"]),
                    new("Coupling is a static Born network", "NP_007: Actualization defines a STATIC COUPLING NETWORK — the interference cross-term 2√(ρ_Aρ_B)·cos(θ_A−θ_B) is the link between any two superposed states, with link weight κ = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B) (DERIVED, Born). The network carries count/information flow (M_005), is reciprocal (D_037), and yields collective modes (in-phase (√ρ_A+√ρ_B)², anti-phase (√ρ_A−√ρ_B)²) — but NO phase flow and NO propagating field (BOUNDARY).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_007"]),
                    new("Extremum principle is hidden in I", "NP_008: canonical Actualization extremizes NOTHING (option D) — the self-rate update θ(t+1)=θ(t)+Δθ drifts I non-monotonically (1.760→0.980). The extrema of I are in-phase max ((√ρ_A+√ρ_B)²) and anti-phase min ((√ρ_A−√ρ_B)²); its gradient ∂I/∂θ_A = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B)·sin(θ_B−θ_A) IS the missing sync term — a variational phase update would lock rel at an extremum. Principle EMERGENT/BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_008"]),
                    new("No hidden objective in actualization", "NP_009: canonical Actualization obeys NO extremum principle (D) — it IGNORES I (drifts 1.760→0.260; no increase/decrease/conservation). No hidden objective exists (count conserved M_005, info static M_004, distinguishability static D_039, I not fed back). Smallest modification: one gradient term θ+=Δθ+η·∂I/∂θ — a gradient flow (d rel/dt=−2ηκ·sin(rel), κ=2√(ρ_Aρ_B)) with stable fixed point rel=0 = max(I) — so actualization WOULD follow max(I) and sync would emerge. EMERGENT/BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_009"]),
                    new("Synchronization needs a second layer", "NP_010: Network 1 (Actualization, local self-rate) cannot synchronize. κ = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B) is a LINK property (depends on both endpoints, symmetric) — DERIVED. But no canonical mechanism carries phase flow (reciprocity is a read basis D_037; info flow redistributes counts M_005; shared events pin once M_002). Network 2 (phase-flow/gradient layer) is structurally present, dynamically absent — BOUNDARY; sync would live there.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_010"]),
                    new("Network 2 is not a physical field", "NP_011: the coupling network (κ = 2√(ρ_Aρ_B)) is a MATHEMATICAL STRUCTURE, not a physical field. It fails all criteria: no state-independent existence (κ=0 without states), no stored structure (no field variables), no information/phase/energy transport, and κ is descriptive only (the canonical update has no κ term). Every observable (interference, collective modes) comes from the state structure alone. A physical coupling field would be BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_011"]),
                    new("Unique predictions are spectral", "NP_012: after removing all QM-equivalent interpretations, the measurement/coupling programs leave NO observationally-testable unique prediction (event, pinning, feedback, log₂95, conservation, observer are all QM-equivalent; AT-P043 downgraded; AT-P042 structural only). The surviving uniquely-AT predictions are the N=96 spectral values: ω₁=√91·(2π/N)≈0.624 (FIRST), families=floor(log₂ span)+1=3, O(2) doublet, v=137·ln(span)=254.37 GeV.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_012"]),
                    new("Strongest D96 prediction is the O(2) doublet", "NP_013: the strongest falsifiable D96-specific prediction is the O(2) EXACT DOUBLET DEGENERACY (λ_k=λ_{N−k} for every mode k, D_021) — absent from QM/GR/SM. Ranking (uniqueness×impact×feasibility): O(2) doublets 13, family count 13, ω₁=√91·(2π/N)≈0.6244 (12), v=137·ln(span)=254.37 GeV (11), span=6.4025 (10). All five are uniquely D96 PREDICTIONS.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_013"]),
                    new("Synchronization is optional", "NP_014: physics does NOT require synchronization (option B). Comparing U1 (canonical, no locking) with U2 (gradient locking), every canonical law survives identically (measurement M_002, conservation Σρ=1 & log₂95, reciprocity D_037, 95 states D_039, identity D_036). U2 only collapses relative-phase diversity (continuum → one). The canonical absence is a FEATURE preserving the information channel.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_014"]),
                    new("O(2) doublets give exact mirror frequencies", "NP_015: the O(2) exact doublet degeneracy (λ_k=λ_{N−k}, D_021) predicts observable mirror-pair frequencies — ω_k/ω_{N−k}=1 exactly, 47 mirror pairs + central mode k=48, O(2) reflection symmetry. Any |Δλ|>0, missing pair, or triplet falsifies it. Distinct from QM (no fixed spectrum), SM (weak doublets are non-degenerate gauge pairs), GR (no frequencies). PREDICTION.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_015"]),
                    new("Mirror pairs are native to the ring", "NP_016: the O(2) mirror-pair degeneracy is native to the D96 ring modes (ω_k=ω_{N−k} for every k≠48, |Δλ|=0). Target ranking: (1) ring resonance spectrum HIGH; (2) cosmological acoustic peaks MEDIUM (peak ratios, D96-derived); (3) GW ringdown LOW (damped); (4) SM particles LOW (weak doublets split); (5) neutrinos LOW (ordering open). Mirror pairs observable only in C96-ring systems.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_016"]),
                    new("CMB carries the D96 octaves", "NP_017: nature contains an APPROXIMATE D96 signature — the CMB acoustic peak ratios follow the D96 octave hierarchy (ℓ₁=220.48, 0.008%; r₂₁=2.4368, 0.035%; r₃₁=3.6965, 0.058%; n_s=0.96497 — QG237/238, CORRESPONDENCE). But no natural system shows the exact O(2) mirror-pair degeneracy (PREDICTION, unobserved). CMB is the strongest candidate; no domain falsified.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_017"]),
                    new("Distinguishability is directly observable", "NP_018: distinguishability generates direct observables — the state count (95, D_039), entropy (log₂95=6.57 bits, M_004), information density (I_occ=0.7513 nats, QG228), and — strongest — the cosmological fraction ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 (QG234, OBSERVED to 0.12%). QM/SM/GR have no fundamental observable as a function of distinguishability.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_018"]),
                    new("Information cosmology is the density pair", "NP_019: the information density I_occ fixes EXACTLY the density-fraction pair — ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839, Ωm=(ln K−I_occ)/ln K=0.3161 — and their ratio 2.1636. n_s (0.96497) and ℓ₁ (220.48) are D96-spectral, not I_occ functions; H₀/σ₈/BAO/growth have no direct relation. I_occ is a genuine but narrow cosmological variable.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_019"]),
                    new("Black holes cannot destroy information", "NP_020: a black hole CANNOT eliminate Difference — information is conserved through horizon formation (M_005). The conserved quantities (count Σρ=1, positivity, normalization, 95-state identity D_039) survive crossing; the horizon removes ACCESS, not distinguishability. Fates: destroyed NO; hidden/redistributed/preserved YES. Mechanism: HORIZON BOOKKEEPING. Resolves the paradox in the conservation direction.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_020"]),
                    new("Horizon bookkeeping: store, redistribute, encode", "NP_021: information is conserved across a horizon by HORIZON BOOKKEEPING — storage (states retain distinguishability, D_039), redistribution (external radiation re-encodes, M_005), encoding (hidden/accessible partition). State-space expansion is REFUTED (state space fixed at 95). Balance: log₂95 = H_hidden + H_observer, conserved.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_021"]),
                    new("Geometry and information share ρ", "QG_001: both geometry and information EMERGE from the count density ρ (option C). Geometry: g = ρ^(2/d)η (QG197/222). Information: I = KL(ρ‖uniform) = 0.7513 nats (QG228), feeding ΩΛ = I_occ/ln K = 0.6839 (QG234). Neither generates the other; the bridge is ρ. Black holes, cosmology, and measurement all confirm the shared root.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_001"]),
                    new("Geometry is a manifestation of distinguishability", "QG_002: the metric g = ρ^(2/d)η is a PURE FUNCTION of the N=96 state structure (N → spectrum → ρ → g). Metric information is inferable from distinguishability alone. AT is INFORMATION-FIRST (distinguishability → spectrum → ρ → metric), not geometry-first. Horizon, ΩΛ, and measurement all confirm the chain.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_002"]),
                    new("Geometry is not informationally complete", "QG_003: information alone cannot reconstruct the metric. I = KL(ρ‖uniform) is a single scalar; ρ is a full distribution (many share the same KL). ΩΛ fixes ln K (the size), not ρ. g = ρ^(2/d)η needs ρ. The chain is state structure → ρ → {I, g}; the inverse fails. The state structure (N=96) is the primitive.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_003"]),
                    new("ρ is fundamentally a count", "QG_004: ρ is a COUNT STRUCTURE (option C) — ρ_k = count_k/total, Σρ_k=1 (Born QG216). Geometry (g=ρ^(2/d)η) and information (I=KL(ρ‖uniform)) are its DERIVED faces. Removal tests: remove geometry → information survives; remove information → geometry survives; remove count → both vanish. Count is the most primitive of the three.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_004"]),
                    new("Geometry is the measurement of the density", "QG_005: geometry is count-derived (option C). The metric g=ρ^(2/d)η is the UNIQUE conformal-flat metric preserving the counting measure (√(−g)=ρ ⟹ k=2/d, QG207) — the ruler required to measure the density's volume. Remove metric → count survives; remove count → geometry undefined. Geometry IS the measurement of the distinguishability density.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_005"]),
                    new("Count conservation is the foundation", "QG_006: count conservation (Σρ=1) is DEFINITIONAL — built into ρ via the normalizer S (ρ_k=μ^k/S, QG194) — and NECESSARY: removing it collapses geometry (√(−g)=ρ), information (KL), measurement (Born), and black-hole bookkeeping (H_before=H_after) SIMULTANEOUSLY, with no 'first'. All are DERIVED from the normalized count.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_006"]),
                    new("Count conservation is forced by Difference", "QG_007: Σρ=1 follows logically from Difference via FINITENESS. Difference → distinguishability (D_039) → finite 95-state space → normalization required for probabilities (Born) and measures (√(−g)=ρ) → conservation. Remove it: the QUALITY survives (states distinct) but info/geometry/measurement fail. No alternative primitives ({Difference, η} only).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_007"]),
                    new("Finiteness is a boundary", "QG_008: finite distinguishability is BOUNDARY — required for physics (finite info, well-defined normalization/measure) but not implied by Difference. The value N=96 is derived (closure); the finiteness is an input. With N→∞, INFORMATION breaks first (log₂ N diverges); normalization/geometry/measurement are second (limit assumptions).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_008"]),
                    new("The infinite is consistent — the uniform reference breaks", "QG_009: an infinite distinguishable state space is INTERNALLY CONSISTENT when the count density is convergent. The geometric ρ_k=(1−r)r^k sums to 1 EXACTLY and carries finite entropy H=2.0 bits (r=0.5); power-law ρ∝k^(−2) normalizes via ζ(2) with H≈2.36 bits. Normalization, measurement (Born), geometry (√(−g)=ρ), and gravity survive. The FIRST genuine failure is the UNIFORM REFERENCE: no normalized uniform measure exists on a countable infinite set, so I_occ=KL(ρ‖uniform) and ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K are ill-defined for infinite N. REFINES QG_008: realized entropy is finite for convergent ρ — only the capacity and the AT KL observable break.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_009"]),
                    new("Observability demands finiteness", "QG_010: observability REQUIRES finite distinguishability. The measurement event is a FINITE act (M_001 — reads both quadratures of one mode) with finite information capacity log₂(N_obs) (M_004 — log₂(95)=6.57 bits/event). An infinite observable alphabet would need log₂(N)→∞ bits per event — impossible for a finite act. The observable state space is DERIVED-finite (largest set one event can resolve); the underlying state-space finiteness remains BOUNDARY (QG_008). Resolves QG_009 OP1: observability, not Difference, pins the finite OBSERVABLE space.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_010"]),
                    new("Finite observation follows from the discrete step", "QG_011: finite event resolution is a CONSEQUENCE of Actualization. An event is ONE discrete tick (Δθ=2πk/N, D_041) producing ONE outcome (M_001) with finite information (M_004). An infinite-resolution event is self-contradictory (infinitely many steps, no single outcome). Finite observation is DERIVED; the discreteness of the actualization tick is the final BOUNDARY. Closes the QG_008–QG_011 finiteness chain.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_011"]),
                    new("ΩΛ is privileged but not alone", "QG_012: distinguishability generates a FINITE FAMILY of cosmological observables — ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839, Ωm=0.3161 (the pair, observed), the ratio 2.1636, the current deceleration q₀=Ωm/2−ΩΛ=−0.5258, and the turnaround redshift z_acc=(2ΩΛ/Ωm)^(1/3)−1=0.6295 (hosted FRW form, derived values). H₀/σ₈/BAO/growth/lensing/clustering are NOT information functions (need anchors, A_s, sound horizon) — no full information cosmology. Refines NP_019.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_012"]),
                    new("Three families: confirmed boundary, anchored by ΩΛ", "QG_013: the 3-family WINDOW is a CONFIRMED BOUNDARY — NOT reducible to pairing (all octave rungs 3·2^k, N=48/96/192/384, are pairing-complete with λ=12 mult 5) or to information (I_occ is monotone in N: 0.524→1.013, no extremum at 3). It is ANCHORED by the observed cosmology: ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 is reproduced EXACTLY only by N=96; N=48→0.477 (−30%), N=192→0.815 (+19%), N=384→0.895 (+31%). The family-count VALUE 3 is DERIVED (floor(log₂ span(96))+1); the WINDOW is BOUNDARY (D_020/D_040).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_013"]),
                    new("Cosmology constrains, it does not select", "QG_014: the observed cosmology is a CONSTRAINT on the observable sector — only the 3-family sector (N=96) reproduces all four observables (ΩΛ=0.6839, Ωm=0.3161, q₀=−0.526, z_acc=0.630); 2/4/5 families deviate by 13–21% (falsified). Not a full selection (the observed ΩΛ is an input, not derived) and not a coincidence (I_occ(96)=0.7513 is exactly the KL of [4,4,87], QG228). Forward direction DERIVED; backward direction conditional.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_014"]),
                    new("A distinguishable universe need not be observed", "QG_015: observability is EMERGENT (the actualization readout, M_001) under a BOUNDARY input (the observable sector, D_020), not a consequence of Difference. U1 (distinguishable, unobserved) is fully consistent: geometry (g=ρ^(2/d)η), information (I_occ=0.7513), and cosmology (ΩΛ=0.6839) all exist without observation; the read adds only realization + observer access (M_005: reveal, not create). Necessity REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_015"]),
                    new("The tick is discrete because it is input", "QG_016: tick discreteness is a BOUNDARY. Difference implies discrete STATES (a discrete set, DERIVED) but NOT discrete EVENTS — continuous actualization with rate ω=2πk/(N·τ) reproduces AT-P042 exactly at every sampled time (M_010), so observability does not force discreteness. The first inconsistency of continuous evolution is STRUCTURAL (the phase advance loses its spectral derivation). The step VALUE Δθ=2πk/N is DERIVED; the stepwise DYNAMICS is input.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_016"]),
                    new("ΩΛ heads a finite, closed cosmology family", "QG_017: ΩΛ is not an isolated success — it is the first member of a FINITE distinguishability cosmology family: ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839, Ωm=(ln K−I_occ)/ln K=0.3161, ratio 2.1636, and (hosted FRW closures) q₀=Ωm/2−ΩΛ=−0.5258 and z_acc=(2ΩΛ/Ωm)^(1/3)−1=0.6295. The family is algebraically CLOSED (I_occ+H=ln K; ΩΛ+Ωm=1). H₀/σ₈/BAO/growth/lensing/horizon/clustering need non-information inputs — no full info cosmology. Strongest next: q₀/z_acc.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_017"]),
                    new("The info-cosmology chain is closed (90%)", "QG_018 (V2.3 closure): the chain Difference → … → ρ → I_occ → {ΩΛ, Ωm, q₀, z_acc} is mathematically CLOSED (score 9/10): acyclic, circularity-free (ln K fixed by QG227), with KL as the UNIQUE information measure reproducing ΩΛ = 0.6839 (squared Hellinger 0.3833, total variation 0.5302, chi-squared 1.3896 all fail). The closure fails only for convergent-infinite N (no normalized uniform measure, QG_009). Exact boundary set: the canonical eight.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-QG_018"]),
                    new("O(2) doublets are full-symmetry; no larger O(2) exists", "NP_023 (V2.3 closure, hostile audit): the D96 spectrum carries per-frequency O(2) doublets (42 × 2D irreps; |Δλ|=0), and the full degeneracy structure is {mirror pairs} ∪ {octave-ladder blocks} (λ=12 five-fold, λ=14 six-fold, D_030) — both structural, symmetry-protected, ZERO accidental degeneracies. Automorphisms stay within gcd classes. A larger O(2) mixing frequencies is FALSIFIED; the Z2 is not a remnant.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_023"]),
                    new("The √(6/7) ratio is the strongest O(2) prediction", "NP_024: the mirror-pair degeneracy is CORRESPONDENCE (generic: rings, QM m↔−m, phonons k↔−k). The uniquely-K=6 prediction is the octave-block structure: a C96-ring resonator must show one 5-fold resonance at ω=√12, one 6-fold at ω=√14, and the exact coupling-independent ratio ω(√12)/ω(√14)=√(6/7)=0.92582. Exceeds NP_022's mirror-pair prediction.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_024"]),
                    new("The √(6/7) ratio is a K-family law, not K=6-specific", "NP_025: √(6/7) is the K=6 member of the universal K-family √(K/(K+1)) — an N-independent protected inter-block ratio of every circulant ring C_N(±1..±K) with K≥2 (K=2: √(2/3), K=5: √(5/6), K=6: √(6/7), K=7: √(7/8), …). The ratio is injective in K, so a measured ratio UNIQUELY identifies K; the multiplicities pin N. Refines NP_024; elevates the prediction to a general law.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_025"]),
                    new("√(K/(K+1)) is a nearest-neighbour circulant theorem", "NP_026: R_K=√(K/(K+1)) holds ONLY for the canonical nearest-neighbour circulant class C_N(±1..±K) with uniform weights (determination B) — alternative generator sets (odd, powers-of-2), weighted links, random perturbations, missing links, and non-circulant graphs all fail. The analytic origin: λ_{N/4}=2K+2 and λ_{N/6}=2K (modes k=N/4, N/6; N divisible by 4 and 6). The block ratio is WEIGHT-FRAGILE, unlike the reflection-PROTECTED mirror pairs.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_026"]),
                    new("The Planck factor form is emergent; the full law is not", "NP_027: the Planck FACTOR FORM n(x)=1/(e^x−1) IS emergent from the D96 geometric occupation statistics (⟨n_k⟩=1/(e^(k·ln(1/μ))−1), QG194), and the UV is regulated by the FINITE spectrum (95 modes, ω∈[0.62,3.98]) — but the FULL Planck law (Stefan-Boltzmann T⁴=π⁴/15, Wien displacement at x=2.821, continuous DOS, Rayleigh-Jeans divergence) does NOT emerge from the finite discrete spectrum without importing temperature (x=ℏω/kT), a non-canonical primitive. The finite UV is spectral-cutoff-derived, not ℏ-quantization; the four continuous limits hold; the discrete 95-mode sum ≠ π⁴/15. Classification: form DERIVED (QG194); temperature dependence BOUNDARY; finite-UV regulator DERIVED; full law NOT REPRODUCED from D96 alone; ℏ-cutoff origin REFUTED for AT. Closure 6/10.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_027"]),
                    new("The D96 spectrum cannot reproduce the observed blackbody", "NP_028: coarse-graining the 95 D96 modes does NOT reproduce the observed Planck spectrum. The D96 cumulative mode count grows sub-power-law ~ω^1.5 (N(2.5)/N(1.0)=8/2=4.0 vs ω³=15.6), NOT the smooth ω² DOS of a 3D cavity. The spectrum is top-heavy — 93.7% of modes above band mid, 87.4% (83/95) in the top 20% of the band [0.622,3.98] — and occupancy weighting at θ=1 leaves 65.7% of energy above ω=3.3 vs 23.2% for Planck in-band. The high-frequency falloff is a HARD cutoff at ω_max=3.98 (density rises into it: 0 modes in [3.0,3.1), 6 in [3.9,4.0)), not the Wien e^(−ω/θ) tail. Classification: per-mode occupation factor CORRESPONDENCE (NP_027 form); blackbody DOS FALSIFIED for D96; Wien tail FALSIFIED (hard cutoff); full blackbody after coarse-graining FALSIFIED; temperature θ BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_028"]),
                    new("AT does not need a fundamental ħ", "NP_029: AT derives every mass and energy from the two anchors (v, m_e) times dimensionless D96 ratios — never invoking ħ. m_u=m_e·Σ√m/√Σm²=2.164 MeV (QG173) and M_Pl=v·A³=1.2234e19 GeV (QG181) compute identically with ħ removed; the canonical ResearchY derivation chain contains no ħ constant (ħ appears only in legacy SI-comparison analyzers). Energy-content ratios equal frequency ratios: m_u/m_e=Σ√m/√Σm²=4.2347. Unlike the irreducible physics anchors v and m_e, ħ fixes no AT scale — its only roles are the SI conventions E=ħω and G=ħc/M_Pl². Classification: ħ as a fundamental constant REFUTED; ħ as the frequency↔energy dimensional bridge BOUNDARY (SI unit-convention import, like c); derived ħ-free mass/energy chain DERIVED.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_029"]),
                    new("No canonical object plays the role of temperature", "NP_030: no object in AT plays the role of thermodynamic temperature. The four candidates are each anti-thermal or an order-only scalar: (1) actualization density (canonical branching μ=2, ρ_{k+1}=2·ρ_k, S=255) GROWS — ρ₇/ρ₀=128, ln μ=+0.6931>0, a would-be β=−0.6931<0 (population inversion, not thermal decay); (2) occupancy disorder is the top-heavy octave record [4,4,87] (occupancy rises 21.75× into the top octave); (3) information density I_occ=KL(ρ‖uniform)=0.7513 nats is a DERIVED order parameter (ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839, QG_234) but a fixed scalar with no scale and no occupation law; (4) spectral crowding rises into the band top (83/95 modes above ω=3.3). Decisively, the Bose occupation n=1/(e^x−1) needs a decaying rate μ<1 (NP_027): the canonical μ=2 gives n_k=1/(2^(−k)−1)<0 — negative occupation, NOT Bose statistics. Classification: temperature as a derived object REFUTED; temperature as the import scale x=ℏω/kT BOUNDARY (unchanged, NP_027/028); I_occ stays DERIVED as an order parameter; Hawking T∝1/R derived in form (QG_184/208) with coefficient BOUNDARY (2π, QG_185/196).", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_030"]),
                    new("AT is one structural layer plus an added occupation law", "NP_031: AT splits into one closed DERIVED structural layer (Difference→Actualization→Spectrum→every structural observable: spectrum [95 modes, band 0.622–3.98], occupancy [4,4,87], I_occ=0.7513→ΩΛ=0.6839, masses, M_Pl, couplings, entropy H=log₂95=6.57 bits — none needs a temperature, and the ResearchY derivation chain contains no SI thermal constant) and an ADDED thermodynamic occupancy layer over the structural modes. No thermal observable derives from structure alone (REFUTED): only state entropy (a count) and the occupation FORM (with a free decay μ<1, NP_027) derive; canonical μ=2 gives negative occupation (inversion). The sole overlap is the occupation statistics ρ_k=μ^k/S — a DERIVED count (A_003/QG194) that is the EMERGENT occupation FORM when read with a free decay. Thermodynamics enters only as mode set + occupation n=1/(e^x−1) + temperature scale x=ℏω/kT (BOUNDARY). Classification: Structure Sector DERIVED; thermodynamics-as-autonomous-sector REFUTED; two-layer architecture DERIVED; temperature BOUNDARY; radiation hosted/FALSIFIED as emergent (NP_027/028).", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_031"]),
                    new("No ring size in the canonical family is thermal", "NP_032: scanning N=8..512 of the circulant family C_N(±1..±6) finds NO thermal-attractor ring size. (1) UV: ω_max→continuum 3.9851 for ALL N (96→3.9796, 512→3.9849, 1024/4096→3.9851) — the cap is a property of the coupling set, not of N, so no ring hosts a Wien tail; (2) DOS: λ_k≈(2πk/N)²·91 gives ω_k≈(2π√91/N)·k, an exact linear dispersion (ratio 1.0000 at N=4096), cumulative N(ω)∝ω; every span≥8 ring (392) has low-DOS exponent exactly 1.00 (2D/3D cavity needs 4.0/8.0 ratio) — no ring reaches it; (3) occupancy top-heavy at every N (first octave=4 for 478/505 N; D96's [4,4,87] is one of 61 rings in the 3-family window [4,8)); (4) thermal occupation needs μ<1, but canonical μ=2 is N-independent. Classification: 'another D_N is a thermal attractor' FALSIFIED (no ω² DOS, no tail, no thermal occupancy at any N); structure N=96 as structure base DERIVED (octave/family window); 1D linear DOS + N-independent UV cap DERIVED (analytic); temperature BOUNDARY; thermodynamics as an added occupancy layer DERIVED (NP_031) — NOT an N-selection; 'structure N≠thermal N' as size dichotomy FALSIFIED (the split is a LAYER split).", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_032"]),
                    new("Heat's statistics emerge from a D96 ensemble; the blackbody does not", "NP_033: an ensemble of D96 systems exchanging occupation DOES generate statistical temperature, Boltzmann weights, and Bose occupations — over the FIXED D96 mode set. Zeroth law: two D96 systems in contact peak total entropy at equal β (S(50/50)=35.7306 > S(35/65)=35.3600 > S(20/80)=34.1459). Max-entropy: the Bose occupation n_k=1/(e^(βω_k)−1) strictly dominates uniform/linear/bottom-heavy at the same energy (S=17.8653 > 15.7097 > 15.2101 > 4.03); the Boltzmann identity ln(n/(1+n))=−βω holds exactly over the D96 modes; the microcanonical occupation marginal is geometric, P(n+1)/P(n)=Q/(Q+M−2). But the observed blackbody does NOT emerge: the ensemble thermalizes occupation over the fixed top-heavy [4,4,87] mode set (bimodal octave energy at every T — 94% low at T=0.3, 91% top at T=10; Σω³/(e^ω−1)=120.70≠π⁴/15; no Wien tail). Classification: statistical T/Boltzmann/Bose EMERGENT from the ensemble (standard max-entropy mechanism); zeroth law DERIVED; single-D96 temperature REFUTED (NP_030); observed blackbody FALSIFIED (mode-set obstruction); hypothesis 'structure single-D96 / thermo ensemble-D96' CONFIRMED for the occupation statistics.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_033"]),
                    new("Bose statistics is sufficient; only the D96 mode set blocks the blackbody", "NP_034: the D96 ensemble produces the EXACT Bose/Planck occupation n(ω)=1/(e^(βω)−1) (NP_033 identity) and that occupation is SUFFICIENT for the blackbody — the observed radiance is the product u(ω)=g(ω)·n(ω)·ω. Factorization: U(1)=12.588 over the 95 modes = Σ m_i·ω_i·n(ω_i) over the 44 distinct freqs; Boltzmann identity exact on every D96 mode. Replacing the D96 occupations with exact Planck occupations is a NO-OP (already exact Bose) and blackbody still fails (Σω³/(e^ω−1)=120.70≠π⁴/15) ⇒ occupation is NOT the obstruction. Replacing only the mode set with the ideal ω² DOS reproduces the blackbody exactly with the same occupation: u=ω³/(e^(βω)−1), π⁴/15=6.4939, Wien displacement x=2.821, Rayleigh-Jeans x², Wien tail ⇒ answer A. Sensitivity: UV cutoff (40.7% of blackbody energy above ω_max=3.98 at β=1, no modes above ⇒ no Wien tail); DOS exponent (D96 cumulative p≈1.0 low/1.51 mid vs 3; a p=1.51 power host integrates to 1.79, <π⁴/15/3); clustering (44 distinct freqs, 8-bin [2,2,2,0,2,2,33,52]); finite count NOT an obstruction (ideal ω²-distributed 95-mode set reproduces in-band blackbody to ~0.05%). Minimal deformation: keep the Bose occupation, change ONLY the mode set (p→3, ω³-redistribute the 95 modes, unbind the band). Classification: Bose occupation EMERGENT (NP_033, unchanged); occupation as blackbody factor SUFFICIENT (answer A — the failure is wholly in the mode set); D96 mode set as blackbody host FALSIFIED (NP_028/033, unchanged); UV cap + DOS exponent + clustering of the D96 mode set DERIVED as the minimal obstruction; finite count NOT an obstruction; additional obstruction NONE (answer B refuted); new primitive/layer NOT REQUIRED (hosted ω² DOS suffices, answer C refuted); temperature β BOUNDARY (unchanged).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_034"]),
                    new("The D96 DOS is 1D because the ring is 1D", "NP_035: the exact structural origin of the blackbody DOS mismatch is DIMENSIONALITY. The D96 ring is a 1D structure — its spectrum is indexed by a SINGLE integer k∈[1,N−1] with an exactly linear low-frequency dispersion: λ_k≈(2πk/N)²·91 ⇒ ω_k≈c·k with c=2π√91/N (ratio →1.000 at N=6144, k=1..4); equal frequency spacing ⇒ N(ω)∝ω, p=1 (1D-chain DOS, g(ω)=const). The octave occupancy [4,4,87] confirms it: low octaves hold 4 modes each (constant 1D density per unit ω); the top octave holds 87 only because the band ends at ω_max=3.98 — the NP_028 mid-band 'exponent ~1.5' is a finite-band cap artifact, NOT an asymptotic DOS exponent. The DOS exponent p EQUALS the number of independent integer mode indices: 1D lattice → p=1, 2D → p=2, 3D → p=3 (lattice-count Weyl law, verified). Origin candidates: A) dimensionality — YES, the origin (one integer index k ⇒ p=1); B) topology — NO (a 1D open chain also has p=1); C) finite mode count — NO (p=1 at N=96..6144); D) circulant structure — NO (p=1 for C_N(±1..±K), K=1..12 — K changes band width, not the index count); E) hosted higher-layer geometry — YES for ω² (the blackbody ω² DOS is the DOS of a genuinely 3D host, hosted content, NP_032). Extensions: larger N → p=1; larger K → p=1; coupled/longer rings → p=1; only tensor products raise p (C_N^⊗2 → p≈2, C_N^⊗3 → p≈3 — adding an INDEPENDENT direction). Minimal ω³ construction preserving the D96 ±1..±6 local rule: the 3D tensor product of three D96 rings, C_96(±1..±6)^⊗3 — separable eigenvalues, ω≈c|k| with k∈Z³, N(ω)∝ω³, DOS∝ω² (verified; integer count matches (π/6)R³). Classification: 1D DOS of the D96 ring (p=1) DERIVED (single index, linear dispersion); exponent p = number of mode indices DERIVED; dimensionality as the origin DERIVED (answer A CONFIRMED); ω² DOS as hosted 3D geometry CORRESPONDENCE (NP_028/034 unchanged); any C_N(±1..±K) ring as blackbody DOS host FALSIFIED (NP_032 unchanged); finite count / topology / circulant structure as the cause FALSIFIED; C_N^⊗3 → N∝ω³ DERIVED (minimal construction). No new primitive; canonical AT unchanged.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_035"]),
                    new("Three D96 rings reproduce the 3D blackbody DOS — as hosted geometry", "NP_036: the observed 3D DOS (N(ω)∝ω³, g(ω)∝ω²) is exactly reproduced by the tensor product D96⊗D96⊗D96 — but as a HOSTED CORRESPONDENCE, NOT as an EMERGENT consequence of a single D96 sector. DOS exponents (verified): D96 (single ring) p=1 (octave doubling); D96⊗D96 p→2 (2D Weyl); D96⊗D96⊗D96 p→3 (3D Weyl: positive-octant ball exponent →3, coefficient →(π/6)R³). Comparison: blackbody DOS g∝ω², free-field 3D counting, and the 3D-cavity spectrum all give N(ω)∝ω³ with the (π/6)R³ coefficient — identical to D96⊗3; 2D gives the wrong (π/4)R² law; Stefan-Boltzmann ∫x³/(e^x−1)dx=π⁴/15=6.4939. Sufficiency (verified): tensor eigenvalues separable Λ=λ_k1+λ_k2+λ_k3 ⇒ ω≈c|k| with k∈Z³ — three independent D96 coordinates are MINIMALLY sufficient for N∝ω³ (fewer axes give R¹ or R²). 'Observed 3D = one D96 axis × three' holds as a construction, but canonical AT is ONE ring (p=1 at every N,K, NP_032/035), the metric g=ρ^(2/d)η is dimension-generic (only d≥3 derived via the (d−2) Einstein factor, QG290), and nothing derives three stacked copies ⇒ CORRESPONDENCE (hosted 3D geometry). Hidden triple-factor structure: A=Σm·#g·occ₂=95·44·87=363,660 (mode count × distinct freqs × top-octave modes of the single ring), cubed to M_Pl=v·A³=1.2234e19 GeV (cube exponent 3.0000, QG181/183) — a FREQUENCY-CONTENT triple, NOT three spatial axes; the three octave families [4,4,87] are octave bands within one frequency coordinate. Classification: DOS exponent p=d of the d-fold D96 tensor DERIVED (Weyl); three independent D96 coordinates → N∝ω³ DERIVED (as a construction); observed 3D DOS as D96⊗3 CORRESPONDENCE (hosted 3D geometry, NP_028/034/035 unchanged); 3D EMERGING from a single D96 sector FALSIFIED (single ring p=1); spatial dimension d=3 as a canonical output FALSIFIED (metric dimension-generic, d≥3 only); hidden triple A → M_Pl=v·A³ DERIVED (QG181/183). No new primitive; canonical AT unchanged.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_036"]),
                    new("The strongest unique prediction is ΩΛ", "NP_022: the strongest genuinely unique AT prediction is ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 — already observed to 0.12%, uniquely tied to distinguishability (QM/GR have no observable as a function of distinguishability), falsifiable by any deviation beyond 0.12%. The strongest structural prediction is the O(2) exact mirror-pair degeneracy (|Δλ|=0, ω_k=ω_{N−k}). Top-10 ranked; V2.3: information-cosmology closures (q₀, z_acc) + O(2) ring search.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_022"]),
                    new("Dark energy ontology: information-bookkeeping, energy hosted", "NP_055: ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 has a DERIVED ontology only as the information-bookkeeping fraction (interpretations B=E): the canonical ρ=[4,4,87]/95 gives I_occ=KL(ρ‖uniform)=0.7513 exactly, I_occ+H=ln K, ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K (excess), Ωm=H/ln K (realized-entropy fraction). Interpretations: A) vacuum energy (w=−1) NOT DERIVED (QG230's Λ∝1/R² ⇒ w=−1/3); B) information content YES (literal); C) occupancy deficit INVERTED (deficit = matter Ωm); D) actualization pressure METAPHORICAL (μ=2 anti-thermal); E) bookkeeping YES (=B). Tension: three incompatible dark-energy EoS (w=−1 hosted, w=−1/3 QG230, w≠−1 legacy fitted). ΩΛ is a successful numerical relation + a derived informational ontology, NOT a physical dark-energy mechanism.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_055"]),
                    new("No unique equation of state", "NP_056: ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 is a SNAPSHOT density fraction with no EoS content. Dynamics are DEGENERATE: the same ΩΛ gives H²(z=1)/H₀²=3.21 (w=−1), 4.46 (w=−1/2), 5.26 (w=−1/3) — a 64% spread. Acceleration is NOT derived: q₀=[Ωm+ΩΛ(1+3w)]/2 needs w<−0.4874 (hosted input); QG230's w=−1/3 gives q₀=+0.158 (decelerating). Candidate w's: w=−1 (hosted, ρ∝a⁰), w=−1/3 (M∝R scaling), evolving w(z) (fitted) — all CORRESPONDENCE. NEW: a time-independent ΩΛ forces w_de=0, q₀=+0.5 — the bookkeeping reading is inconsistent with acceleration. AT predicts only a present-day fraction, not a dark-energy dynamics.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_056"]),
                    new("ΩΛ is the normalized entropy deficit", "NP_057: ΩΛ physically represents the normalized ENTROPY DEFICIT — the information surplus of the realized D96 occupancy over the uniform prior — and is a STATE DESCRIPTOR (order parameter), not a cause and not an energy density. A=C=E (surplus = unused state-space = bookkeeping); B (deficit = H/ln K = Ωm) is MATTER; D (pressure) is a metaphor (μ=2 anti-thermal). Order parameter: ΩΛ=0 (uniform), 0.6839 ([4,4,87]), 0.8938 ([1,1,93]) — monotone in top-heaviness. Equivalent forms: I_occ/ln K = KL/ln K = (ln K−H)/ln K = 1−Ωm. Entropic/informational family, not vacuum energy. The information→energy bridge is an empirical CORRESPONDENCE (OPEN).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_057"]),
                    new("The information→energy bridge originates at QG89", "NP_058: the mapping 'information budget → cosmological density budget' originates at QG89, 'energy = its Noether conjugate, measured as the actualization rate' — the FIRST energy language, and a DEFINITION, not a derivation. Inherited at QG230 ('I_vac>0 ⇒ ρ_Λ>0'; 'Λ=8πG·ρ_Λ', importing G=ħc/M_Pl²) and realized at QG234 ('ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K'). Every step before the bridge (ρ→H→I_occ→I_occ/ln K=0.6839) is pure counting/information (DERIVED, no ħ/c/G). Removing the bridge loses nothing on the information side. ΩΛ is an INFORMATION observable (DERIVED) and an energy observable only by CORRESPONDENCE.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_058"]),
                    new("'energy = actualization rate' is a boundary definition", "NP_059: QG89 cannot be DERIVED — it is BOUNDARY (irreducible definition). What IS derived is the conserved COUNT: Σρ=1 (QG194/QG216/M_005) and the deficit Σm=Σ(ρ̄−ρ)=0 (QG194's 'Noether count'). The step from 'conserved count' to 'energy' has no Noether route: AT's time is DISCRETE (Δθ=2πk/N per tick, 'time IS the tick count'), QG244's Lagrangian presupposes QG89's 'actualization-flow energy', and the dimensionful conversion needs anchors (v, m_e, ħ, c). Deepest open step: QG89's identification of the conserved count with energy + the anchored conversion.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_059"]),
                    new("ΩΛ cannot perform work", "NP_060: ΩΛ=I_occ/ln K=0.6839 is a state descriptor, NOT a resource. It fails all three resource requirements (no energy scale — dimensionless; no gradient — single snapshot; no temperature channel — NP_030). Extraction channels: vacuum work d(ρV)=−p dV (hosted, no derived EoS); information work W=k_B T ln 2 (needs T, absent); free energy F=U−TS (needs U and T). Nothing flows under extraction (I_occ, H invariant; Σρ=1, Σm=0 conserved counts). Inversion: the extractable side is Ωm (matter=deficit), not ΩΛ (surplus).", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_060"]),
                    new("The 0.12% match is a fragile point-correspondence", "NP_061: the match ΩΛ=0.6839 vs ΩΛ_obs=0.6847 is a CORRESPONDENCE with a BOUNDARY ingredient. Sensitivity: K=2/4/5 → 0.5801/0.6263/0.5732 (rung ladder 0.4773/0.8153/0.8945); occupancy [5,4,86]→0.6555, [2,2,91]→0.8145; measure only KL matches (Hellinger 0.1917, TV 0.5302, χ² 1.3896). Not necessary, not emergent (fragile), not numerological (no free parameter). Precision rests on the KL measure (EMERGENT choice) and the K=3 window (BOUNDARY, 'anchored by observed ΩΛ', QG_013) + the hosted QG89 bridge. Deep relation REFUTED; coincidence REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_061"]),
                    new("High order is the mandatory shape of the discrete spectrum", "NP_062: reality is highly ordered because ρ is the normalized occupancy of a discrete 1D circulant spectrum — top-heavy BY CONSTRUCTION (linear dispersion ω_k≈c·k + finite UV cap; ~92% top-octave share at N=48/96/120/192). Uniform/random → ΩΛ≈0 (H=ln 3 max); canonical [4,4,87] → H=0.3473 (32% of capacity), I_occ=0.7513, ΩΛ=0.6839. High order is TYPICAL and REQUIRED (structural), NOT dynamically selected (N→occupancy bijection). [4,4,87] is a selection outcome, not an attractor/fixed point. Earliest source of the surplus: the DISCRETE SPECTRUM.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_062"]),
                    new("Two faces of one count density (descriptor correspondence)", "NP_063: ΩΛ_AT=0.6839 (information) and ΩΛ_obs=0.6847 (cosmology) are equal because AT POSITS they are two faces of one count density ρ — a DESCRIPTOR link (B) via an unproven hidden common origin (E). Links: causal REFUTED, descriptor YES (presupposes bridge), scaling law REFUTED (point equality), coincidence PARTIAL (fragile), hidden origin UNPROVEN. Four non-derived assumptions: KL measure (EMERGENT, first), N=96 window (BOUNDARY), 'energy=actualization rate' (QG89, deepest), anchors (BOUNDARY). ΩΛ_obs is itself a measurement. No causal link, no scaling law, no derived deep relation.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_063"]),
                    new("Canonical structure: period-3 seed × 3-family window", "NP_064: {N=96, K=3, [4,4,87]} is a DERIVED-BOUNDARY hybrid. The period-3 seed (DERIVED) forces N=3·2^k and 6|N; the 3-family window [4,8) (BOUNDARY, anchored to ΩΛ_obs) selects k=5 → N=96. Verified: among octave rungs 3·2^k, only k=5 (N=96) has span 6.4025∈[4,8) (k=4→3.24 2-fam, k=6→12.78 4-fam). From N=96 everything is DERIVED: span 6.40 → K=3 → [4,4,87] → I_occ=0.7513 → ΩΛ=0.6839. Removal of N, K, or occupancy breaks ΩΛ (and mass content). True root: period-3 seed (derived) × 3-family window (boundary); deepest boundary below: the discrete tick.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_064"]),
                    new("Dark matter = the matter deficit (an effect, not a particle)", "NP_065: Ωm = H/ln K = 0.3161 is the realized-entropy fraction (the bookkeeping complement of ΩΛ), matching Ωm_obs = 0.3153 to 0.26%. It is read as the matter deficit m = ρ̄ − ρ (QG194): conserved (Σm = 0), positive in the under-occupied low octaves [4,4] (0.2912 each) and negative in the over-occupied top octave [87] (−0.5825) — matter is under-density, dark energy is over-density. Readings: C (realized fraction) = D (complement) = B (matter deficit); A (physical matter) = hosted energy (E_def = m, QG89) + DERIVED gravity. Ontology: matter = deficit (QG194), T_μν = (ρ̄−ρ)v_μv_ν (QG195), flat rotation α=0 (QG206), M∝R (QG184) — all DERIVED, an effect not a particle, no CMB/structure role (QG229). ASYMMETRY: the deficit gravitates (DERIVED), the surplus describes (no derived effect, NP_060) — Ωm has STRONGER physical meaning than ΩΛ, though both share the hosted QG89 energy reading; 'dark matter' as a particle REFUTED; baryonic/dark split BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_065"]),
                    new("Dark matter evidence: the deficit reproduces gravity, not particles or lensing", "NP_066: explanatory power PARTIAL and partitioned — 2 DERIVED / 2 CORRESPONDENCE / 2 REFUTED. Reproduces the gravitational-potential half: flat rotation (v²∝r^(−α), flat⇔α=0, QG206) DERIVED; CMB matter fraction Ωm=0.3161 (0.26%) DERIVED; cluster mass (Coma 6.7× baryon) CORRESPONDENCE — degenerate with ΛCDM; LSS seed+growth (Poisson δ_i, deficit dust δ∝a) CORRESPONDENCE — power-spectrum shape hosted.                     fails the particle/light-bending half: lensing REFUTED (conformal ρ-only metric gives PPN γ=−1, no bending — needs the ψ tensor sector); Bullet Cluster REFUTED (the deficit is a scalar field, not a collisionless particle — no separation from the shocked gas). The deficit is a gravitational-potential surrogate, not a full dark matter.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_066"]),
                    new("Lensing fails because the metric is conformally flat", "NP_067: the ρ-only metric g = ρ^(2/d)η is CONFORMALLY FLAT — PPN γ = −1, so the null-geodesic prefactor (1+γ)/2 = 0 and all lensing observables (deflection, convergence, shear, magnification) plus the Shapiro delay vanish — while potential effects (rotation, cluster, redshift, Ωm) survive because they depend on g₀₀ alone. The origin: the conformal factor scales time and space equally, and null geodesics are conformally invariant. The minimal fix is the ψ TENSOR SECTOR (the second primitive): g₀₀ = −ρ^(2/d)e^(2ψ) breaks conformal flatness ⇒ γ = +1 ⇒ full lensing, weak/strong lensing, Shapiro, frame dragging (QG186), and GW (QG43/44) restored at GR strength (QG212). Lensing needs only a scalar ψ (1 d.o.f.); spin-2 is required only by GW polarization. Primitive cost: +1 (ψ). Determination: B (missing tensor sector) — not fatal, not correspondence-only.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_067"]),
                    new("ψ is the spin-2 graviton — the tensor face of Difference", "NP_068: ψ is the spin-2 (Weyl-curvature) graviton, the tensor (traceless) face of the founding Difference — a PRIMITIVE physical degree of freedom (massless spin-2, 2 polarizations), not auxiliary, not hosted, not emergent. Interpretations: A) geometry correction PARTIAL (completes the metric); B) information field NO (ρ is the information face, ψ the geometric face); C) curvature field YES (the Weyl/traceless curvature conformal flatness lacks); D) physical d.o.f. YES (the graviton) — so ψ = C = D realized as A, not B. Status: emergent REFUTED (spin-0 cannot source spin-2, QG19); primitive YES (second primitive, QG223). Difference Duality (QG286/301): ρ = trace (conformal), ψ = traceless (Weyl curvature) — two faces of one Difference.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_068"]),
                    new("Expansion is the branching growth of the count", "NP_069: what expands in AT is the actualization count ρ — the branching flow ρ_{k+1} = μ·ρ_k, ∂_t ρ = ln(μ)·ρ (QG222) — which carries the metric g = ρ^(2/d)η (the metric scale IS ρ^(2/d), A = B). The FRW scale factor a = ρ^(1/d) (QG77) is a HOSTED relabeling of the same count; q₀/z_acc are hosted FRW closures. At criticality (μ=1) the mean is static (∂_t ρ = 0), only the variance grows (Var = k·σ², the residual pressure QG230 maps to Λ) — no native accelerating scale factor. Earliest growing object: the branching count ρ_k = μ^k/S. Classification: branching + metric scale + native dynamics + redshift DERIVED; FRW a = ρ^(1/d) CORRESPONDENCE (hosted); accelerated expansion HOSTED; a native accelerating scale factor REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_069"]),
                    new("Reality is critical because μ=1 is uniquely selected", "NP_070: μ=1 is the UNIQUE branching ratio that is simultaneously marginal-stable (non-extinct, non-runaway), scale-free (L=1/|ln μ|=∞, renormalization-invariant), and maximum-entropy (α=0) — three criteria coinciding at μ=1 (QG7). μ<1 extincts (q=1, finite total), μ>1 runs away (q<1, exponential), only μ=1 is marginal (linear). μ≠1 breaks scale-freeness (α≠0), hence flat rotation. Criticality is DERIVED (unique), conditional on scale-freeness (renormalization invariance, the indifference principle AT-F1) as the single BOUNDARY input. Chain: Q-events → critical branching (μ=1) → α=0 → ρ → gravity. Classification: criticality DERIVED; scale-freeness BOUNDARY; α=0 DERIVED; sub/supercritical universe REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_070"]),
                    new("Matter is the deficit — a stable self-bound excitation", "NP_071: matter is the DEFICIT m = ρ̄ − ρ — a stable, self-bound EXCITATION (a deficit pattern) of the count density, the under-occupancy that sources gravity and clumps. Interpretations: point objects REFUTED; occupancies YES; resonant structures PARTIAL (substrate); stable wave structures YES (= B, the founding README claim 'matter consists of dynamically stabilized wave structures' made precise); solitons LEGACY (superseded TRM reading). Category: pattern + excitation (the corpus's 'deficit excitation'), not substance, not process. Stability: only the converging (deficit) branch clumps — matter = deficit is DERIVED from STABILITY (ATF MatterAttraction). Classification: matter = deficit DERIVED (QG194); mass ladder DERIVED (QG173/209); particle REFUTED; soliton LEGACY; fundamental substance REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_071"]),
                    new("Particles are resonance classes; the electron is the octave-bottom anchor", "NP_072: a particle is a RESONANCE CLASS — a mode (frequency attractor) of the D96 spectrum, organized into octave-band families, with mass = anchor × dimensionless D96 ratio. Interpretations: localized deficits NO (the deficit is bulk matter); resonance classes YES; occupancy configurations YES (= B, the mode-access classes of [4,4,87]); measurement observables PARTIAL. Matter vs particle: matter = the deficit over the modes; a particle = a single mode. Emergence: spectrum/families/mass-ratios DERIVED; m_e = 0.511 MeV BOUNDARY (anchor). The ELECTRON = the lightest fermion mode (octave bottom, occ₀=4), its mass the boundary anchor; muon/tau/quarks are derived ratios. Classification: modes/families/ratios DERIVED; m_e BOUNDARY; localized point particle REFUTED; fundamental REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_072"]),
                    new("Only 3 modes become particles: family structure + stability + symmetry", "NP_073: only 3 of the 95 D96 modes appear as particle generations because the spectrum organizes into 3 octave bands = 3 families (QG210), and within each band only the STABLE bottom mode survives (metastability, QG125), selected by its quantum numbers via isospin-constrained mode access (QG150, r=0.955). The top band (87/95 = 91.6%) is the bulk deficit, not individual particles. Selection rules (all DERIVED): occupancy (3 bands), symmetry (isospin, down=full-spectrum/up=dense-band), stability (higher modes decay), family structure (no 4th because span<8). Classification: family structure + mode access + metastability DERIVED; 'all 95 modes are particles' REFUTED; particle→label assignment PARTIAL (QG271).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_073"]),
                    new("Quantum numbers are D96 symmetry charges", "NP_074: quantum numbers are the GENERATORS of the D96 automorphism group — symmetry charges (C) that act as occupancy-access rules (B). Gauge symmetry = 1+3+8 = 12 generators (the 12 link-directions of C_96(±1..±6)). Charge = U(1) = Z_96 rotation (the photon charge) DERIVED; isospin = SU(2) = Z2 doublet (T3 = σ_z) DERIVED; color = su(3) = 3²−1 = 8 (structure DERIVED, count 3 = BOUNDARY postulate, QG79); hypercharge = Y = Q−T3 (derived combination). The electron's charge (−1) is its U(1) rotation eigenvalue, not a SM label. Classification: charge/isospin/color-structure/hypercharge DERIVED (QG161); color count BOUNDARY (QG79); 'SM labels only' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_074"]),
                    new("Forces are D96 generator actions", "NP_075: a force is the ACTION of a D96 symmetry generator — a symmetry action (B) that induces a resonance transition between modes (D, vertex ⟨f|T^a|i⟩). EM/weak/strong = the 1+3+8 = 12 generator actions (QG161/243); gravity = the metric geometry (ρ + ψ), NOT a gauge force. Gauge bosons are symmetry generators (link excitations, QG57), not matter particles; the photon = the U(1) = Z_96 rotation generator (e = √(4π/137)). Each force is a Noether-conserved generator action with a D96-normalized coupling. Classification: gauge forces/bosons/couplings DERIVED; gravity DERIVED + ψ primitive; 'force = matter-particle exchange' REFUTED. Forces share the ontology of particles and quantum numbers.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_075"]),
                    new("ψ is not dark matter", "NP_076: the ψ graviton sector contributes ZERO dark-matter mass. Partition: the deficit carries the mass (rotation/cluster/Ωm = 0.3161), ψ carries the optics (lensing γ=+1, frame dragging, GW). ψ produces A) propagating waves only (massless spin-2); B) stationary bound configurations REFUTED (m_ψ=0, no Yukawa binding); C) effective mass density REFUTED (w=1/3, ρ ∝ a⁻⁴, Ω_gw≈10⁻⁹ ≪ Ωm). ψ fails every dark-matter requirement (cold/pressureless/a⁻³/Ω=0.316/clustering/collisionless). ψ's role is to restore lensing so the deficit's mass is visible — the deficit is the dark matter, ψ is the graviton. Dark Matter = DEFICIT-ONLY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_076"]),
                    new("Structure forms only with extra assumptions", "NP_077: the deficit field produces the seed and linear growth of structure DERIVED (Poisson δ_i = 1/√⟨N⟩, scale-free variance Var(2k)/Var(k)=2, pressureless dust, δ ∝ a, n_s = 0.96497 — QG231/237), but halos/galaxy profiles/clusters form only with EXTRA ASSUMPTIONS. The flat-rotation halo profile (v²≈const ⇒ M∝r ⇒ ρ∝r⁻², the SIS) requires the α=0 log-deficit abundance law = a SYMMETRY SELECTION (PREFERRED/BOUNDARY), not a dynamical attractor (G4-RHO: conservation→repulsive ρ∝r⁻², scale-freeness→continuum). The SIS is steeper than the NFW cusp (r⁻¹) and singular (no core); NFW concentration FITTED; clusters match ΛCDM degenerately (Coma 6.7×). Determination: B.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_077"]),
                    new("α=0 is the unique scale-free point", "NP_078: the universe selects α=0 because it is the UNIQUE point where flat rotation (v² ∝ r^(−α), slope 0 only at α=0), stability (equal-deficit-per-octave; α<0 diverges, α>0 concentrates), criticality (μ=1 ⟺ α=0), and maximum entropy coincide. The α-scan [−2,+2]: only α=0 gives a flat curve and M ∝ R (exponent 1−α = 1). α=0 is DERIVED (unique) as a SELECTION fixed point (RG fixed point of the octave renormalization), NOT a dynamical attractor (G4-RHO: conservation→repulsive ρ∝r⁻², scale-freeness→continuum). Earliest source = scale-freeness = AT-F1 indifference principle = the FINAL boundary (not derived).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_078"]),
                    new("Scale-freeness is derived from Difference", "NP_079 (refines NP_078): scale-freeness is NOT the final boundary — it is DERIVED from Difference. Difference is a BINARY (all-or-nothing) distinction carrying no metric/magnitude/scale, so the primitives it grounds (Q-events, the counting measure as a density of weight d, the causal order as a scale-invariant partial order) carry no intrinsic scale. Scale-freeness = renormalization invariance = the unique power-law (RG fixed point) = indifference (AT-F1), all one statement. A finite intrinsic scale λ breaks μ=1 and α=0; λ→∞ recovers self-similarity. The true final boundary is DIFFERENCE itself (QG270), not scale-freeness.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_079"]),
                    new("Difference has exactly two faces", "NP_080: Difference splits into exactly ONE scalar face (ρ) and ONE tensor face (ψ) because it actualizes into a SYMMETRIC rank-2 object (A_ij = A_ji) whose decomposition is exhaustively spin-0 TRACE (1 = ρ, count/magnitude/isotropy) ⊕ spin-2 TRACELESS (5 = ψ, orientation/Weyl, 2 TT polarizations). 6 = 1 + 5 at d=3: no third component, no vector (spin-1) face, no spin-≥3. Remove ψ → conformally flat (no lensing/GW); remove ρ → no count (no information/Ωm/ΩΛ). The duality is DERIVED (the rank-2 decomposition theorem); primitive cost = 1 (Difference, with ρ and ψ as its two faces).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_080"]),
                    new("Energy is a relabeling, not fundamental or emergent", "NP_081: energy inside AT is a RELABELING of actualization dynamics. The conserved object is the COUNT (Σρ = 1, Σm = Σ(ρ̄−ρ) = 0 — DERIVED); 'energy' is that count renamed (QG89 'energy = actualization rate', a DEFINITION) and unit-ized (anchors v, m_e + ħ, c). Noether fails (discrete time, no native Lagrangian). Removing energy language loses nothing derived: ΩΛ = I_occ/ln K = 0.6839 is pure information, masses are spectral ratios, forces are generator actions. Determination: E (boundary definition) = C (as a definition) on A (count density); B (flow) and D (emergent) REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_081"]),
                    new("The electron mass is a replaceable unit conversion", "NP_082: m_e is NOT the true remaining matter-scale boundary — it is a REPLACEABLE unit conversion. All fermion mass ratios are DERIVED (dimensionless: m_μ/m_e = Σm²/√occMom = 207.03, m_τ/m_μ = √occMom·λ₂ = 16.842); the absolute scale m_e = 0.511 MeV carries only the DIMENSION (MeV), which no derived D96 invariant can supply (span, occupancy, count, A = 95·44·87 are all dimensionless). No derived quantity can replace m_e; only another dimensionful scale (v or M_Z) can, which just relocates the same single boundary. The true boundary is 'one dimensionful scale' (irreducible); m_e is its replaceable instance. Determination: D (unit conversion only).", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_082"]),
                    new("The two 3s share the period-3 seed", "NP_083: color-3 and family-3 share a HIDDEN COMMON DERIVATION (B): both descend from the period-3 seed p = 3 (DERIVED, D_040), which forces the factor 3 in N = 3·2^k → 3 octave bands → 3 families (value DERIVED) AND the su(3) color algebra (8 = 3²−1 from the 3 family directions). The color count 3 is the octave-3 identification (QG79, BOUNDARY). Two boundary residues remain (family window [4,8), color identification). Removing family-3 breaks su(3)+masses+ΩΛ; removing color-3 breaks baryons but keeps family-3. Not independent boundaries, not a single boundary — one DERIVED seed + two identifications.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_083"]),
                    new("η is the reference that makes the reading possible", "NP_084: η is the conformal reference metric — a FRAMEWORK boundary, irreducible and necessary, but not a physics primitive. It defines the trace (ρ), the traceless part (ψ), conformal flatness (Weyl = 0), the Weyl content, the metric g = ρ^(2/d)η, and PPN γ. Removing η breaks the entire geometric reading (no trace, no traceless, no conformal flatness). It is not a coordinate convention, not a hidden background metric, and not derivable from Difference — the contraction Tr(A) = η^ij A_ij presupposes η, so {Difference, η} = content + reading are genuinely two. The trace/traceless duality (NP_080) is DERIVED given η.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_084"]),
                    new("The completeness frontier", "NP_085 (frontier map): DERIVED — foundations, D96 structure, information (ΩΛ = 0.6839, Ωm = 0.3161), matter (deficit), particles (resonance classes, mass ratios, quantum numbers, forces), scalar gravity. CORRESPONDENCE (hosted) — the energy reading, cosmic acceleration, lensing/GW (via ψ), cluster mass, weak/strong couplings, Bose statistics. BOUNDARY — the equation of state w, temperature, and seven irreducible inputs plus one dimensionful scale. MISSING — condensed-matter physics and nuclear structure. REFUTED — the blackbody and the Bullet Cluster.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_085"]),
                    new("Difference is the minimal primitive", "NP_086: Difference is the MINIMAL and UNIQUE primitive — the bare logical possibility of distinction, the weakest notion that generates counting and everything downstream. It is not derivable and not replaceable: identity is insufficient (self-identity a = a does not give a ≠ b; Leibniz's identity of indiscernibles makes distinction require a difference), relation presupposes distinct relata, and count/information/symmetry are downstream (they presuppose distinguishable states). Identity is the logical dual (a ≠ b ⇔ ¬(a = b)) but the preservation relation used for conservation, higher in the chain, while Difference is the generation relation at the bottom. No weaker primitive exists — distinction IS difference.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_086"]),
                    new("Nuclei are correspondence-only", "NP_087: nuclei are CORRESPONDENCE-ONLY. The nucleons are DERIVED — the proton (uud) and neutron (udd) are quark composites forming an isospin doublet — and the strong coupling is a spectral correspondence (α_strong = 8/Σ√m). But the nuclear structure is MISSING: binding energies (deuteron 2.224 MeV, helium-4 28.3 MeV), the magic numbers [2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126], and the shell structure are all absent. The reason is structural: the D96 ring is one-dimensional (47 mirror-pair degeneracies), while nuclear shells are three-dimensional spherical harmonics closed by spin-orbit — the 1D ring does not generate the 3D shells.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_087"]),
                    new("The theory already networks to 3D", "NP_088 (refines NP_087): the theory is NOT confined to a single 1D ring — it ALREADY contains an emergent 3D geometry: the tensor product D96 ⊗ D96 ⊗ D96 raises the DOS exponent to p = 3 (a cubic lattice), and the dimension d = 3 is DERIVED (QG197's (d−2) bridge selects d ≥ 3 for non-trivial gravity). D96 is a seed AND a node (Weyl law p = d). But this does NOT rescue nuclear structure: the cubic lattice has octahedral symmetry (irreps of dimension 1, 2, 3), while nuclear shells need rotational symmetry (2l+1 = 1, 3, 5, 7) closed by spin-orbit — the cubic lattice breaks the spherical degeneracies, so the magic numbers still do not follow. NP_087's '1D' diagnosis was too narrow; its 'missing' verdict survives, now refined to cubic-vs-rotational symmetry.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_088"]),
                    new("Rotational symmetry is approximate only", "NP_089 (refines NP_088): O(3) is APPROXIMATE ONLY in the cubic network. The free dispersion ω² = k² − (k_x⁴+k_y⁴+k_z⁴)/12 + … is isotropic to leading order (the k² term), but the cubic correction (k_x⁴+k_y⁴+k_z⁴) breaks O(3) with an O((ka)²) anisotropy (~0.5% at ka = 0.3) that is suppressed yet never zero at any finite lattice spacing. Because the theory is discrete (N = 96), the exact continuum limit (a → 0) is never reached, so the exact 2l+1 spherical-harmonic degeneracies are split (5 → 2+3, 7 → 1+3+3) at every scale. Nuclear structure therefore remains missing — the reason refined once more to 'O(3) is only approximate'.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_089"]),
                    new("The network is the organized difference structure", "NP_090: the D96 network is the ORGANIZED DIFFERENCE STRUCTURE. Nodes = distinctions (Difference events = actualizations — a Q-event is a before→after difference); links = the adjacency (the symmetric rank-2 connectivity A_ij = A_ji); what propagates = the 95 resonance modes (the particles) and the two faces ρ (the trace → the metric g = ρ^(2/d)η) and ψ (the traceless → the curvature). Concrete: 96 nodes × degree 12 = 576 links, trace Σλ = 1152. Particles are modes of ONE D96 ring; the tensor-product network supplies the 3D space. Geometry is emergent from connectivity, not embedded.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_090"]),
                    new("The network becomes spacetime in three steps", "NP_091: the D96 network becomes spacetime in three steps — network (nodes = distinctions, links = adjacency, path = a chain of links) → geometry (g = ρ^(2/d)η = the trace face of the connectivity; ψ = the traceless/Weyl face = the curvature) → spacetime (3D space EMERGENT via D96⊗D96⊗D96 with d=3 DERIVED through the (d−2) bridge; +1 time = the actualization TICK, the discrete causal order, hosted as the +1 Lorentzian signature, a FRAMEWORK residue). ONLY SPACE IS EMERGENT; time is the framework tick. ρ does double duty: metric conformal factor AND cosmic expansion (a = ρ^(1/d)). First NON-DERIVED step = {Difference, η} + the tick.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_091"]),
                    new("Nothing travels — propagation is the tick and the light cone", "NP_092: propagation in AT is NOT transport — the network is STATIC (nodes and links do not move), and nothing substantial travels node to node. The ONLY genuine movement is the actualization TICK (the causal-order advance along the generation relation, massless null M_eff = 0, FRAMEWORK), and its native propagation law is LIGHT ALONG NULL GEODESICS (n = 1, independent of ρ, DERIVED). Everything else only APPEARS to move: a particle is a resonance mode (a standing wave) whose localized wave-packet ENVELOPE travels at the group velocity v_g = dω/dk (EMERGENT); the graviton is a ripple in ψ; a force is link-mediated generator action; the count ρ redistributes by continuity ∂_t ρ + ∇·j = 0. Velocity = v_g (≤ c, subluminal at finite k); locality = the adjacency; causality = the partial order.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_092"]),
                    new("The Born rule is the node-selection law", "NP_093: if nothing travels (NP_092), what selects the node is the BORN RULE — at each tick, one count is realized on one node, selected with probability ρ_k = |ψ_k|² (the conserved, normalized count share, Σρ = 1 EXACT). The two real DOFs select differently: the PHASE θ = 2πk/N advances DETERMINISTICALLY per tick (Δθ = 2πk/N, D_041) and does NOT select the outcome; the COUNT ρ realizes PROBABILISTICALLY with Born weight and IS the selector. Competing maxima resolve statistically (the larger-ρ node wins proportionally, not always). Matches Born statistics (QG216), the M_001 measurement, and the double-slit buildup exactly. The weight is DERIVED (count conservation → normalization → Born rule); the only boundary is the irreducible STOCHASTIC REALIZATION — one outcome per tick — the discrete tick (the deepest single boundary).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_093"]),
                    new("Inertia is resonance persistence", "NP_094: inertia = RESONANCE PERSISTENCE — the stability of a spectral eigenmode under free (generator-free) actualization. Motion is NOT object transport (nothing travels, NP_092) but resonance propagation: a particle (resonance mode, NP_072) has a wave-packet envelope advancing at v_g = dω/dk. Momentum = the PHASE GRADIENT k (θ = 2πk/N, p = ℏk); mass = the REST FREQUENCY ω₀ = m/ℏ; force = a GENERATOR ACTION (a resonance transition, NP_075) that changes k. Free actualization (tick + Born + deterministic phase, D_041) does not change k; only a generator does. So Newton I (F=0 → v=const) is the network statement that a mode's phase gradient is conserved under free actualization. The electron (ω₀>0) has inertia and can rest; the photon/graviton (ω₀=0) have no rest inertia and always propagate at n=1.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_094"]),
                    new("Friction is the opposite of inertia", "NP_095: friction = RESONANCE SCATTERING realized as MODE MIXING (B = C) — the incoherent accumulation of generator actions (resonance transitions, NP_075) between a propagating mode and the deficit excitations (matter, NP_071) in its path. Each scatter changes the wave number k (momentum); the aggregate redistributes k into the material's modes (dissipation) while preserving the resonance class ω₀ (identity, elastic). Friction scales with matter density n (γ ∝ n, dk/dt = −γk → k(t) = k₀e^(−γt)): ZERO in vacuum (n=0 → k conserved, pure inertia), growing gas → liquid → solid. This is the OPPOSITE of inertia (NP_094): inertia = no resonance transitions (k conserved); friction = resonance transitions (k changes); both reduce to the single generator action — once (a force) or many times (friction).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_095"]),
                    new("Heat is random phase; entropy is its measure", "NP_096: heat = RANDOM PHASE = MODE MULTIPLICITY = RESONANCE DECOHERENCE (A = B = D), realized as COUNT REDISTRIBUTION (C) under count conservation (NP_081). Friction's lost phase gradient k becomes incoherent (random-phase) mode excitation spread over the material's many modes. Entropy is its measure: H = −Σρ ln ρ — the mode multiplicity (0 for one mode, ln 95 = 4.5539 for all 95). Entropy growth IS increasing mode access. Heat erases information: I_occ = KL(ρ‖uniform) = ln K − H → 0 as the count spreads toward uniform. One ontology: motion (coherent phase, NP_094) → friction (scattering, NP_095) → heat (random phase) → entropy (mode multiplicity).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_096"]),
                    new("Temperature is the count-distribution width", "NP_097: temperature = the COUNT-DISTRIBUTION WIDTH (T = ∂U/∂S) — the thermodynamic conjugate of entropy, where U is the conserved count (energy, NP_081) and S = H = −Σρ ln ρ (entropy/mode multiplicity, NP_096). DERIVED from the occupancy ρ alone: cold (T=0.2) concentrates the count in 2 modes (H=0.0407), hot (T=100) spreads it over all 95 (H=4.5171). H and the occupancy width monotonically track T; I_occ = ln K − H falls. The thermodynamic identity dS/dU = β is exact. Phase randomness (B) is HEAT, not temperature; the scattering rate (C) is a consequence. The dimensionless temperature is DERIVED; the absolute Kelvin scale is BOUNDARY (the k_B unit anchor, the same 'one dimensionful scale' pattern as m_e and v).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_097"]),
                    new("Localization is constructive interference", "NP_098: localization = the CONSTRUCTIVE INTERFERENCE of a WAVE PACKET — a superposition of resonance modes whose coherent addition peaks |ψ|² = ρ at one node and cancels elsewhere. A particle is a propagating resonance (B); its localization is the Born distribution's envelope peak (C); its trajectory the actualization chain (D); a single localized node (A) is refuted. Fourier uncertainty Δx·Δk = 1: a single mode (Δk→0) is delocalized (|ψ|² = 1/N uniform), a wave packet (Δk large) is localized. Position = the envelope peak; time = the tick (the envelope advances at v_g); the Born selection realizes one node with probability |ψ|². Wave packet + Born weight DERIVED; localization (position/trajectory) EMERGENT; the node basis FRAMEWORK.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_098"]),
                    new("Classicality is decohered localization", "NP_099: classicality = DECOHERED LOCALIZATION (A) — a localized resonance (wave packet, NP_098) is quantum while its interference term survives (I = ρ_A + ρ_B + 2√(ρ_Aρ_B)cos Δθ); friction (NP_095) randomizes the phase until γ·t ≫ 1, when ⟨cos Δθ⟩ → 0 and the fringes vanish → the object follows a single non-interfering worldline. Decoherence (A) is the mechanism, realized through repeated actualization (B) on a stable resonance hierarchy (C), with entropy dominance (D) the signature. Macroscopic bodies (~10²³ modes) decohere instantly. The classical limit (ℏ→0) is the decoherence-dominant limit (γ·t ≫ 1) = the large-N/high-entropy limit. Classicality is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_099"]),
                    new("Binding is resonance locking (deficit clustering)", "NP_100: binding = RESONANCE LOCKING = PHASE SYNCHRONIZATION = DEFICIT CLUSTERING (A = B = D), held by PERSISTENT GENERATOR ACTION (C, the binding force). A bound state is a STABLE MUTUAL CONFIGURATION of resonances whose relative phase is LOCKED (constant across ticks); it persists because the lock is a stable fixed point of actualization (the bound-state analogue of inertia, NP_094). Cascade: modes → pair (e⁻+p) → atom (hydrogen, 13.6 eV) → molecule (H₂), each a deficit clustering (NP_071) of the level below. Free = delocalized (drifts); localized = wave packet (transient); bound = locked (persistent). Binding DERIVED; the bound structure EMERGENT; binding energies BOUNDARY (the m_e-anchor pattern).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_100"]),
                    new("One scale-free stability principle builds the hierarchy", "NP_101: ONE universal, scale-free stability principle explains nested structure across scales — a stable structure is a DEFICIT CLUSTERING (matter, the unique self-bound branch, NP_071) locked at a GENERATOR-BALANCED FIXED POINT (resonance locking = phase synchronization, NP_100). A = B = C = D. Because it is scale-free (AT-F1, NP_079), it repeats self-similarly from 10⁻¹⁵ m (particle) to 10²¹ m (galaxy) — 36 orders of magnitude — producing particle → atom → molecule → crystal → planet → galaxy, each a deficit clustering of the level below. The principle is DERIVED; the hierarchy is EMERGENT; specific sizes/energies are BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_101"]),
                    new("Existence is a Difference that endures", "NP_102: to exist = to be a PERSISTENT, DISTINGUISHABLE structure — a Difference (a distinction, NP_086) that endures actualization (stability, NP_094/100). Existence has exactly two conditions: DISTINGUISHABILITY (the thing is a Difference, the minimal primitive) and STABILITY (it persists across ticks). A = B = C: actualization persistence = stable resonance = bound deficit structure; D (observability) is a CONSEQUENCE, not the condition (the states pre-exist measurement, M_005). Remove stability → everything dissolves into momentary actualizations, only Difference survives. Difference is BOUNDARY; stability DERIVED; the hierarchy EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_102"]),
                    new("Nonexistence is the loss of Difference or persistence", "NP_103: nonexistence = the NEGATION of existence — a thing ceases to exist when it loses its DISTINGUISHABILITY (Difference) or its PERSISTENCE (stability): NOT(Existence) = ¬Difference ∨ ¬Persistence. Decay (¬Persistence) and thermalization (¬Difference) are the full channels; losing localization or binding is PARTIAL (form, not existence). Information is REDISTRIBUTED, not destroyed (count Σρ = 1 conserved, M_005). The final stage of decay is structure → pattern → noise → UNIFORM (ρ_k = 1/K, maximum entropy) → DIFFERENCE (the substrate). Nonexistence reaches uniform noise, never nothing.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_103"]),
                    new("Difference cannot cease", "NP_104: Difference is INDESTRUCTIBLE and CONSERVED — it cannot cease. Complete uniformity is uniform OCCUPANCY (ρ_k = 1/K), still with K=95 distinct modes. Difference cannot decay (a transition between distinct modes), cannot thermalize (uniformizes the occupancy, not the distinctions), and cannot 'become uniform' (uniform count still has distinct modes) — every process presupposes the distinctions it operates on. There is no state with no distinctions; 'Difference ceased' is itself a difference. Difference is BOUNDARY (the ground); its distinguishability is DERIVED-conserved (M_005); its disappearance is REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_104"]),
                    new("Difference is the parent conservation law", "NP_105: Difference is the TRUE conserved quantity — the PARENT conservation law (A = B = C; D refuted). The invariant is the indestructible Difference structure (95 distinct states, NP_104); every conserved quantity projects from it: count (Σρ = 1) is the MEASURE, information is the STRUCTURE, momentum/charge are the Noether symmetries (1+3+8 = 12 D96 generators). Existence is NOT conserved (it can cease, NP_102/103). Removing Difference breaks everything. Conservation of Difference is BOUNDARY; the projections (count/information/momentum/charge) are DERIVED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_105"]),
                    new("Difference must actualize — it is an act, not a state", "NP_106: Difference must actualize because a Difference is an ACT of distinguishing, not a static state. A 'static Difference' is a contradiction: an un-drawn distinction is no distinction (it collapses into undifferentiated unity, refuted by NP_104). Therefore actualization is a LOGICAL NECESSITY (C) of Difference — to differ is to enact the distinction, and the tick is that enactment. Actualization is NOT an independent primitive (B refuted); it is the dynamic face of Difference. Removing actualization leaves nothing (no counting, information, structure, or existence). Only the discreteness of the tick is the BOUNDARY residue (QG_011). The minimal reason: a distinction must be drawn to be a distinction.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_106"]),
                    new("Ticks follow from the binary nature of Difference", "NP_107: actualization occurs as discrete ticks because Difference is BINARY ('this ≠ that') — a discrete cut, not a continuum. The tick's discreteness is a LOGICAL NECESSITY (A), DERIVED from the binary nature (the act of drawing the distinction realizes one side per act). Continuity fails at the very first step, and counting, Born selection, localization, and the phase lattice all inherit the discreteness. A 'continuous Difference' is a contradiction (blurs the cut into undifferentiated unity). This REFINES QG_011: the deepest boundary is the BINARY NATURE, not the tick's discreteness. Discreteness DERIVED; binary nature BOUNDARY.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_107"]),
                    new("AT is decisively falsifiable", "NP_108: AT is FALSIFIABLE at every level. Strongest (tightest) falsifiers: the single-valued DERIVED numerics — n_s = 0.96497 (0.007%), ℓ₁ = 220.48 (0.008%), ΩΛ = 0.6839 (0.12%), Ωm = 0.3161 (0.26%), the mass ratios, and the pre-registered 0νββ value (m_ββ = 2.02 meV). Strongest uniquely-AT falsifier: the discrete tick (AT-P042). Weakest: the ontological claims. Strongest vulnerability: nuclear structure (O(3) approximate only — magic numbers not exact). The anchors and w = −1 are BOUNDARY (imported). 'AT is unfalsifiable' is REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_108"]),
                    new("The weakest link is nuclear structure", "NP_109: AT's weakest link is NUCLEAR STRUCTURE (O(3) approximate only — the magic numbers are NOT exactly reproduced), with condensed matter the largest unmapped domain. Foundations/particles/forces are ROBUST; cosmology/gravity are PARTIAL (numerics and metric DERIVED, but w = −1 HOSTED and ψ a framework boundary); nuclear/condensed are MISSING. Strongest evidence = the tight cosmology numerics (n_s = 0.96497, ℓ₁ = 220.48, ΩΛ = 0.6839); weakest = nuclear. The single most-likely falsifier = an exact magic-number closure from the cubic lattice (AT predicts it cannot be exact).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_109"]),
                    new("Condensed matter is the next layer, not beyond D96", "NP_110: condensed matter = the NEXT NATURAL LAYER above binding (NP_100) and hierarchy (NP_101), NOT beyond D96. A crystal = a repeated bound network (a lattice of phase-locked deficit clusters); a phonon = a collective mode (the low-k resonance, ω = 2c·sin(ka/2) ≈ c·k); a magnet = a phase-locked network; a superconductor = global phase coherence. A = B = C; D (new ontology) refuted. No new primitive/geometry/symmetry — only LARGER networks. The first failure point = the cubic O_h anisotropy (the same O(3)-approximate weakness as nuclear structure, NP_089). DERIVED (ontology) / EMERGENT (layer) / PARTIAL (quantitative).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_110"]),
                    new("An observer is a distinguisher within the network", "NP_111: an observer = a PERSISTENT DIFFERENCE STRUCTURE (matter/existence, NP_071/102) that ACTUALIZES (reads) the distinctions of its environment. Observation = A = B = C = D: actualization (state selection, M_001) = information acquisition (log₂(95) bits, M_004) = difference recognition (D_039) = persistent-structure interaction. Particle < detector < observer (a continuous chain); observers are special only in integration scale, not in kind. This resolves M_001 OP1: the observer IS an actualization subsystem. Observer DERIVED; the integrating hierarchy EMERGENT; 'fundamentally special' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_111"]),
                    new("Reality is structure-relative", "NP_112: a persistent observer experiences a stable reality because BOTH observer and world are persistent Difference structures in the same ontology, and decoherence makes their encounter consistent. Reality = A = B = C = D: the sequence of actualizations (A) integrated (B) by the observer recognizing persistent structures (C), which is the emergent narrative (D). Two stabilities multiply: the world persists (inertia/binding/hierarchy, decohered to one worldline) and the observer persists (a bound hierarchy) → repeated readings agree. Reality is STRUCTURE-RELATIVE (persistent structures reading persistent structures), neither observer-independent nor observer-relative. The narrative is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_112"]),
                    new("'Observed' means incorporated into the observer", "NP_113: 'observed' = a distinction INCORPORATED INTO A PERSISTENT OBSERVER (D) — a persistent Difference structure (NP_111) has changed its own structure to include the distinction. Distinct from 'actualized' (a node realized by Born selection, NP_093 — universal, no observer), 'localized' (the wave-packet peak, NP_098 — a state-property), 'measured' (a read of both quadratures, M_001), and 'recorded' (a persistent trace). Determination D; A (actualized) and C (distinguished) necessary not sufficient; B (localized) neither. Observation changes the observer and the phase (M_002), not the structure (reality is revealed, not created). Chain: actualization → localization → measurement → observation → recording.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_113"]),
                    new("Knowledge is predictive integrated distinctions", "NP_114: knowledge = STABLE, INTEGRATED, PREDICTIVE DISTINCTIONS (B = C = D) — the persistent information held by an observer as an anticipatory structure, realized via stored observations (A). Chain: Difference → observation (NP_113) → memory (NP_102) → knowledge (the predictive integration). Knowledge is DISTINCT from information (the distinguishability structure, M_004) and observation (the single event): its differentiator is PREDICTION — it shapes the observer's future actualizations. Removing memory leaves observation but collapses knowledge and understanding. Knowledge is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_114"]),
                    new("Understanding is the model of models", "NP_115: understanding = the MODEL OF MODELS (D) = the COMPRESSION of knowledge (B) = a PREDICTIVE HIERARCHY (C) — knowledge raised to self-reference: the observer holds a compressed model of its own predictions, so it knows WHY it predicts (not merely WHAT next) and can re-derive, transfer, and generalize. A (more knowledge) is REFUTED. The separator from knowledge is compression + self-reference. Understanding changes future actualization more effectively (generalizes to novel cases). The chain: data → information → observation → memory → knowledge → understanding; detector < recorder < predictor < understander. Understanding is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_115"]),
                    new("Meaning is the significance of a distinction", "NP_116: meaning = VALUE-WEIGHTED PREDICTION = the relation between models (C = D) — the SIGNIFICANCE of a distinction for an observer, i.e., how much it matters for the observer's future actualizations. It is the apex of the epistemic chain (data → information → observation → knowledge → understanding → meaning), distinct from all four below it by its SIGNIFICANCE dimension. A (interpreted information) and B (contextual understanding) are necessary but not sufficient. Removing context or prediction collapses meaning; information survives. The minimum condition is significance. Meaning is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_116"]),
                    new("Purpose is the selected prediction (the aim)", "NP_117: purpose = the SELECTED PREDICTION (B = C = D) — meaning PLUS selection: the prediction the observer picks as its aim. Meaning WEIGHS (NP_116); purpose SELECTS a direction and AIMS the observer toward it. A (weighted meaning) is necessary but not sufficient — the differentiator from meaning is SELECTION. The teleological chain: observer → meaning (weighs) → purpose (aims) → action (does). Removing value or prediction collapses purpose. Purpose CHANGES future actualization — it directs the observer's subsequent actions. Purpose is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_117"]),
                    new("Choice is the act of selecting", "NP_118: choice = the ACT OF SELECTING (A = C = D) — constrained actualization = purpose becoming action: the observer selects one alternative, value-guided, toward its aim. B (value maximization) is PARTIAL. Choice is DISTINCT from purpose (the aim) and action (the result). The agential chain: meaning (weighs) → purpose (aims) → choice (selects) → action (does). The minimum condition for a genuine choice is ALTERNATIVES + VALUES + PURPOSE — separating choice from raw actualization (a draw vs. a selection toward an aim). Choice is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_118"]),
                    new("Freedom is selection under incomplete determination", "NP_119: freedom = the CAPACITY TO SELECT AMONG MEANINGS (C = D) — actualization under incomplete determination: the value-weighted, purpose-directed selection whose outcome is not predetermined but weighted. A (number of alternatives) is partial; B (unconstrained) is REFUTED — freedom is value-constrained. Freedom is the middle path between determinism (forced) and randomness (random): chosen action. It is compatible by identity with the foundations — freedom IS Born selection under incomplete determination (weight = value, draw = the tick), and it selects without creating (Difference conserved). Freedom is EMERGENT, on the BOUNDARY tick.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_119"]),
                    new("Responsibility is freedom plus persistence", "NP_120: responsibility = FREEDOM + PERSISTENCE — the ownership of the action and its consequences (A = B), grounded in the continuity of the observer (D), which is the persistence of choice (C). The same observer who chose (freedom, NP_119) is the same observer who bears, because the observer is a persistent Difference structure (NP_102/104). Responsibility is DISTINCT from freedom (capacity), choice (act), and action (result): its differentiator is CONTINUITY. Forced and random actions bear no responsibility; only free action does. The moral chain: meaning → purpose → choice → freedom → responsibility. Responsibility is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_120"]),
                    new("Identity is the sameness of the persistent structure", "NP_121: identity = the SAMENESS of a persistent Difference structure (A = D) — the continuity face of existence: not just that the thing IS (NP_102) but that it is THE SAME across ticks. Identity is grounded in the persistence of the STRUCTURE, not the persistence of content: the observer's actualizations, memories, and states change every tick, but its Difference structure persists (NP_104), so it is the same observer. B (memory) and C (actualization pattern) are PARTIAL correlates. The minimum condition for 'the same observer' is persistence. Identity is distinct from existence (being) and responsibility (owning — which presupposes it). Identity is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_121"]),
                    new("Consciousness is the recursive self-model", "NP_122: consciousness = the RECURSIVE SELF-MODEL (D) = SELF-OBSERVATION (B) — the observer's model turned on itself, so it observes its own observing. A (observation) is necessary but not sufficient (a detector observes without being conscious); C (integrated observation) is partial. The epistemic chain culminates in recursion: observation → knowledge → understanding → identity → consciousness (incorporate → predict → model → persist → SELF-MODEL). The minimum condition is the self-model: an observer is conscious exactly when it observes that it observes. NO new primitive — consciousness is the same Difference ontology turned recursive. Consciousness is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_122"]),
                    new("Truth is correspondence, not success", "NP_123: truth = CORRESPONDENCE (C = B = D) — the stable mapping of the observer's model's distinctions onto the world's persistent Difference structures. A (successful prediction) is necessary but not sufficient (a prediction can succeed by luck; 'useful' ≠ 'true'). Truth is DISTINCT from knowledge (prediction), understanding (model of models), and meaning (value-weighted prediction): its differentiator is CORRESPONDENCE. True model = corresponds; useful model = predicts well; false model = contradicted. Removing prediction leaves truth partially intact; removing observation or consistency collapses it. The minimum condition is correspondence (stable, consistent). Truth is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_123"]),
                    new("Wisdom is responsible action guided by understanding", "NP_124: wisdom = RESPONSIBLE ACTION GUIDED BY UNDERSTANDING (D) — the integration of the prior epistemic/teleological/agential/moral levels into right action. B (balanced understanding) and C (truth-weighted purpose) are necessary but not sufficient; A (accumulated knowledge) is REFUTED (a knowledgeable fool exists). Wisdom is the apex of the chain knowledge → understanding → meaning → purpose → wisdom: it knows the truth, understands why, values what matters, and owns what it does. Removing truth, meaning, or responsibility collapses wisdom while knowledge and understanding survive. The minimum condition is integration. Wisdom is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_124"]),
                    new("Cooperation is aligned purposes and mutual models", "NP_125: cooperation = ALIGNED MEANINGS = SHARED PURPOSES = INTEGRATED SELF-MODELS (A = B = C) — multiple persistent observers whose values (NP_116) and aims (NP_117) align, mutually modeling each other. D (optimization) is PARTIAL. Cooperation is the multi-observer extension of individual purpose (one aim → many aligned aims); conflict is the misaligned counterpart. Freedom is RETAINED (aligned choice) and responsibility is DISTRIBUTED. The social extension: consciousness = model of SELF (NP_122); cooperation = model of OTHER + alignment. Cooperation is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_125"]),
                    new("Resonance sailing is radiation pressure, not a reactionless drive", "NP_126: resonance sailing = RADIATION PRESSURE (DERIVED) — a bound structure (NP_100) phase-locks to a propagating mode (photon/graviton/ψ wave) and gains its momentum k (NP_094): absorption +k, reflection +2k. The transfer is a resonance transition (NP_075). A spacecraft rides a network wave as a sail rides the wind (P = 2I/c; 9.07 μN/m² at the solar constant). Momentum is CONSERVED (transferred, not created); causality and thermodynamics hold. A reactionless drive is REFUTED (it would violate momentum conservation).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_126"]),
                    new("Resonance engineering is coherence engineering", "NP_127: the Difference → Resonance ontology uniquely suggests COHERENCE ENGINEERING — technologies that manipulate mode properties (frequency, phase, coupling), because matter IS a bound resonance (NP_100). The four levers are all valid (A = B = C = D): enhanced coupling, reduced scattering, coherence preservation, and controlled phase locking. Allowed applications: propulsion (resonance sailing), communication (phase as the information channel), sensing (mode-spectrum fingerprinting), energy (low-scattering coherent coupling), and materials (phase-locked crystals, NP_110). Rejected (conservation violations): reactionless drive, perpetual motion, FTL, and information destruction. No new physics, only new leverage.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_127"]),
                    new("Coherence is a physical resource", "NP_128: coherence IS a physical resource — a MEASURABLE RESOURCE (A) and an INDEPENDENT physical capability (D): the phase-lock's persistence (NP_100), which enables interference, coupling, and phase-locked work. B (bookkeeping) and C (hidden information) are PARTIAL — coherence correlates with information (I_occ = ln K − H) but is also the phase alignment, not just distinguishability. Coherence is DISTINCT from information, energy, and entropy: it is the ORDER (locked phase) those quantities presuppose. It is a DEGRADABLE resource with a fundamental trade-off coherence ↔ entropy: friction (NP_095) converts coherence (low entropy) into entropy (high entropy, NP_096), so coherence has a finite lifetime (γ⁻¹), a density, and a transfer cost — exactly as work degrades to heat. Every capability (motion NP_094, binding NP_100, sailing NP_126) is coherent phase-locking, and all five technologies (communication, sensing, power, materials, propulsion) benefit from maximizing it — the most practically valuable quantity.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_128"]),
                    new("Phase engineering outperforms thermal processing", "NP_129: bound matter can be created, modified, and dissolved through coherent phase control — and phase engineering OUTPERFORMS thermal processing for material transformation. Because binding = phase-locking (NP_100) and heat = decoherence (NP_096), a structure is a set of phase-locked modes, and the lever that holds it (phase) can, applied resonantly, also build or break it. Thermal melting is mode-blind (equipartition over N modes, ΔS = N·ln2 bits, efficiency 1/N); coherent disruption is mode-selective (one resonant quantum E_bind into the target lock, ΔS = ln2 bits, efficiency ~1). The advantage is N× in energy efficiency and entropy reduction (~95× for N = 95, the D96 count), at the cost of coherence (NP_128) — a favorable exchange. Conservation laws and the 2nd law HOLD: coherent control deposits energy (no free lunch), transfers momentum (NP_075), and is MORE reversible (lower ΔS), never less.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_129"]),
                    new("Matter is a writable resonance score", "NP_130: a material can be represented by a coherent resonance signature, and the inverse signature can modify it — matter is a WRITABLE RESONANCE SCORE ('material music'). Because binding = phase-locking (NP_100), a material's fingerprint is its mode spectrum {(ω_i, A_i, φ_i)} — one point in a ≥2^95 ≈ 3.96×10²⁸ identity space. Sonification is a bijective scale map of modes onto the audible band (~212.6 Hz/mode for N=95 onto 20 Hz–20 kHz). Invertibility is EXACT for the full fingerprint (round-trip ~10⁻¹⁴) and PARTIAL for frequency-only (phase lost). Targeted coherent excitation (NP_129) writes the score — soften / reshape / disorder / re-order — at one resonant quantum per mode, an N× (~95×) energy and entropy advantage over thermal melting. Crystal and metal leverage clean/high-Q modes; glass and granite are moderate. 'Material music' is a perceptual scale map of a physical spectrum, not literal sound.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_130"]),
                    new("Materials have a resonance master key", "NP_131: every material possesses a small set of CRITICAL resonance modes — the load-bearing backbone — whose coherent excitation drastically reduces macroscopic rigidity. Because binding = phase-locking (NP_100), rigidity is carried by a percolating backbone of m ≪ N critical locks, not uniformly by all N modes. The backbone is material-specific: crystal/metal m = 6 (~15.8× energy advantage over thermal), granite m = 20 (~4.8×), glass m = 40 (~2.4×). Rigidity is collective: unlocking one of 6 critical modes drops R 1.00 → 0.67, and the full set drives R → 0. Coherent critical-mode excitation spends m resonant quanta (m·E_bind) vs thermal N·E_bind, with entropy m·ln2 ≪ N·ln2 — a transient, gel-like, REVERSIBLE softening (re-lock restores rigidity), not a melt.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_131"]),
                    new("Softening is reversible, melting is not", "NP_132: coherent excitation of critical modes produces a REVERSIBLE low-rigidity state without thermal melting — materials can be temporarily softened and reshaped without conventional heating. Softening and melting are DISTINCT. Softening drives the m critical (backbone) modes open, dropping rigidity R → 0 while the lattice order S SURVIVES (the N−m modes stay locked) — a gel-like (unjammed) solid with entropy ΔS = m·ln2 bits. Melting heats all N modes, dropping R → 0 AND order S → 0 — a disordered liquid with ΔS = N·ln2 bits. Softening is REVERSIBLE: stop the drive, re-lock the m modes, rigidity returns (no re-nucleation, because order was never lost). Melting is IRREVERSIBLE: order must be re-grown (N·E_bind + nucleation barrier). Reversibility advantage N/m (~15.8× crystal/metal, ~4.8× granite, ~2.4× glass).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_132"]),
                    new("Rigidity control is ultrasonic-scale", "NP_133: the critical rigidity modes live in the AUDIO-to-ULTRASOUND band (~kHz–MHz), so coherent softening is an ULTRASONIC-scale technology (audio for meter-scale structures), not a THz-scale one. The critical modes are long-wavelength acoustic phonons with fundamental frequency f_1 = c_s/(2L): ~25–27.5 kHz for a 10 cm sample and ~2.5–2.75 kHz for 1 m. The Debye frequency f_D = c_s/(2a) ~ 8.3–9.2 THz is the individual-BOND mode, a factor ~10⁸× higher and physically distinct. Resonant coupling therefore favors ultrasound (audio for large structures); THz is ~10⁸× off-resonance for rigidity and couples to bonds (heating/chemistry, NP_096). Existing ultrasonic technologies (piezoelectric transducers, horns, SAW devices, phased arrays) can excite these modes.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_133"]),
                    new("Discover the master key by rigidity ranking", "NP_134: the critical rigidity modes of an unknown material are found by RIGIDITY-PERTURBATION RANKING — measure the spectrum (NP_130), sweep a resonant drive across each mode, record the rigidity response R(e_i), and rank by influence I_i = |∂R/∂e_i|; the top-m modes are the master key. The diagnostic signatures are C (coherence sensitivity) and D (nonlinear coupling): the critical modes are those whose decoherence or nonlinear drive most collapses rigidity (NP_131). A (amplitude) is REFUTED (uncorrelated with load-bearing role); B (phase) is PARTIAL. Backbone influence ≈ 1/(1−p_c) vs ≈ 0 for non-backbone. Crystal/metal show a sharp gap (m = 6); amorphous glass a diffuse gap (m = 40). Minimum measurement: a single stiffness-vs-frequency sweep.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_134"]),
                    new("A decisive experiment for coherent softening", "NP_135: the critical-mode softening hypothesis is falsifiable by a simple matched-power experiment — drive a 10 cm bar (aluminum/quartz/steel) with ultrasound OFF-resonance (thermal control) and ON-resonance (the critical mode f_1 = c_s/(2L) ≈ 25–29 kHz) at equal power, measuring rigidity via resonant-frequency shift, damping via Q, and ΔT. Define R = ΔR(f_1)/ΔR(f_off). The hypothesis predicts R ≫ 1 (a large, reversible, frequency-selective excess beyond the thermal baseline ΔR/R₀ = −α·ΔT ≈ 0.015–0.045 %/K), with order surviving (no melting). The experiment falsifies the hypothesis iff R ≈ 1 (the drop depends only on ΔT). The off-resonance drive is the built-in thermal control, so any reversible excess is coherent.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_135"]),
                    new("Variable rigidity is bounded only by failure modes", "NP_136: the maximum reversible rigidity reduction is bounded by the failure modes, not the softening mechanism — coherent critical-mode control is a TRUE variable-rigidity technology (10–90%+ reversible), not a minor 1% effect. The rigidity ratio follows percolation R(x) = max(0, (1−x−p_c)/(1−p_c)): for ordered materials (crystal/metal/ceramic, m = 6), one critical mode unlocked gives ~33% reduction, two ~67%, three ~100% (gel); polycrystalline granite (m = 20) steps in ~10%. So 10%, 50%, and 90% are all achievable (90% near-gel, unloaded), while 1% is below the step size (a thermal-scale effect). The maximum is bounded by fracture (loaded vs unloaded), heating (N/m efficient, ΔT small), and decoherence (order S ≈ 1).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_136"]),
                    new("Real-world evidence partially supports coherent softening", "NP_137: the existing laboratory record PARTIALLY supports coherent critical-mode softening. Across seven phenomena (resonant ultrasound spectroscopy, acoustic softening, nonlinear mesoscopic elasticity, phononic crystals, ultrasonic welding, acoustic fluidization, dynamic modulus reduction), three are grade B (partially consistent) — acoustic softening, nonlinear elasticity, acoustic fluidization; two are grade C (thermal) — welding, dynamic modulus; RUS and phononic crystals are neutral tools. The signature (frequency-matched, athermal, reversible acoustic softening) is already OBSERVED — Langenecker's acoustoplastic effect lowers flow stress without matching heating and re-hardens on removal. The magnitude (quantized 33–100% elastic steps) is UNTESTED: observed reversible elastic bound ΔE/E ≈ ΔG/G ≤ 0.30, below the predicted 0.33 step, while the 0.50–0.90 effects are plastic flow stress, not rigidity. No experiment shows a reversible order-preserving R→0 gel. The decisive gap: NP_135's matched-power experiment must read the elastic modulus, not the flow stress.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_137"]),
                    new("Softening is continuous, not quantized", "NP_138: resonantly driven materials do NOT exhibit the discrete modulus steps NP_136 predicted — the quantized ladder ΔE/E ∈ {0.33, 0.66, 1.00} is REFUTED. The staircase R(x) = max(0, (1−x−p_c)/(1−p_c)) with p_c = 0.5, m = 6 gives three 33.3% steps, but every resonant/acoustic elastic-modulus dataset is CONTINUOUS in its control variable (nonlinear mesoscopic elasticity, acoustoplastic, DMA). Discrete steps exist in nature only via (a) thermal first-order phase transitions — spin-crossover ~71% (7→2 GPa) at T_c, ferroelastic precursor, α–β quartz — at arbitrary values, and (b) plastic slip avalanches P(S) ~ S⁻ᵗᵃᵘ, in the wrong channel and statistics. This satisfies NP_136's own falsification condition ('drops continuously, not in ~33% steps'). The staircase was a DERIVED artifact of the small-integer m = 6 idealization; the physical truth is the continuous, athermal, reversible softening (a CORRESPONDENCE, NP_137). The 'master key' is a continuous dial, not a three-position switch.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_138"]),
                    new("The critical modes are the soft modes", "NP_139: the surviving NP_131–138 mechanism is an EXTENSION (B) — a new interpretation and extension of known soft-mode physics, not genuinely new physics. All four AT ingredients map onto known phenomena: AT's 'critical modes' are the Cochran–Anderson soft modes; 'rigidity controlled by a small mode set' is the soft elastic constant (C′ = (C₁₁−C₁₂)/2) / soft acoustic phonon; 'frequency-matched reversible softening' is acoustic softening (acoustoplastic) and nonlinear mesoscopic elasticity; 'continuous softening' is the amplitude-dependent modulus reduction (NP_138). The extension is operational, not physical: a fixed-temperature, order-preserving (S ≈ 1), coherent variable-rigidity dial — whereas the canonical soft mode softens with temperature and culminates in a phase transition. Strongest correspondence: soft-mode theory + the premartensitic C′ anomaly (laser-based RUS). Strongest discrepancy: T-driven phase transition vs a fixed-T order-preserving dial. The mechanism is anchored in real, well-understood physics; the open question is whether the extension survives NP_135's decisive experiment.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_139"]),
                    new("The fixed-T dial is real but small", "NP_140: a large, reversible, fixed-temperature elastic-rigidity dial is PARTIAL — its principle is supported, its magnitude is not. Drive-induced fixed-T softening exists: acoustic softening gives a large (0.50–0.90) but PLASTIC (flow-stress) reduction, and nonlinear mesoscopic elasticity gives an ELASTIC (modulus) reduction of only ~0.01–0.30 (typically ~0.01–0.10), slow-reversible and in disordered/granular media. Large (≈1.00) elastic softening is temperature-driven (soft mode / ferroelastic / martensitic) and terminates in a phase transition, not a fixed-T order-preserving dial. Against NP_136's 10/50/90% tiers, only 10% has partial fixed-T elastic support; 50% is plastic-only and 90% is temperature-only. The elastic modulus is a collective (long-wavelength) property, not a few discrete modes. NP_136's '10–90% variable-rigidity technology' should read as ≤ ~30% for fixed-T elastic rigidity. The decisive test remains NP_135, now expecting a small-to-moderate, continuous, slow-reversible fixed-T modulus drop.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_140"]),
                    new("Coherent excitation is a yield-stress technology", "NP_141: coherent excitation reduces yield stress far more strongly than elastic modulus — it is primarily a YIELD-STRESS technology, not a rigidity technology. Elastic modulus E (a bond property, collective) softens ≤ ~30% (typically ~1–10%) at fixed T; yield stress σ_y (a defect property, gated by dislocation motion) softens 20–90% via preferential acoustic absorption at dislocations. A 10% E reduction accompanies a 2–9× larger σ_y reduction. Practical levers — forming (20–50%), machining (15–40%), drilling, cutting, stone shaping — are all yield-stress or fracture processes. The 'critical modes' that matter are dislocation/defect modes, not the elastic backbone of NP_131. This refines the NP_131–140 chain: the 'resonance master key' is a lever on plastic yield, not elastic rigidity.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_141"]),
                    new("The master key is broadband amplitude, not phase coherence", "NP_142: coherent material control is NEITHER a single-frequency problem NOR a multi-band phase-coherence problem — it is a broadband, amplitude-driven defect coupling (PARTIAL). The coupled objects are independent dislocations with a broad, damped resonance spectrum (Granato–Lücke); single-tone works (amplitude is the lever) but multi-tone is more effective (broader spectral coverage). Of the three axes {ω_i}, {A_i}, {φ_i}, amplitude dominates, frequency (spectral coverage) comes second, and phase third — phase relations do NOT matter more than frequency matching. There is no small discrete mode set (NP_138); the minimum is broadband amplitude above the dislocation breakaway threshold. Adaptive phase feedback helps only for a sharp (elastic soft-mode) resonance. This refines NP_128: for the yield channel, the resource is energy density, not phase coherence.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_142"]),
                    new("Softening is laboratory-accessible", "NP_143: coherent material softening is LABORATORY-ACCESSIBLE, not industrial-scale only. The Granato–Lücke dislocation breakaway threshold ε_c ≈ 10⁻⁶–10⁻⁵ (microstrain) corresponds to a stress σ_c = E·ε_c ≈ 0.06–2 MPa and a power density I_c = σ_c²/(2ρc) ≈ 1–5 W/cm², for aluminum, steel, quartz, and granite alike. Standard equipment crosses this comfortably: piezo transducers deliver ~1–10 W/cm², ultrasonic horns ~10–20+ W/cm², and a 20 kHz horn's 5–20 μm displacement gives 5–1000× the breakaway strain. Industrial scale is required only for throughput, not for threshold access — so the decisive NP_135 matched-power test is buildable with standard lab ultrasonic equipment.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_143"]),
                    new("Every material has a defect fingerprint", "NP_144: every material has a unique, measurable defect fingerprint (Bordoni dislocation peak in aluminum, Snoek interstitial peak in steel, grain-boundary/Kê and NME features in granite/quartz — mechanical spectroscopy), but exploiting it for targeted yield-stress control gives only modest gains. The mobility response is broadband and amplitude-dominated (NP_142); sensitivity is amplitude > bandwidth > frequency. Tailored excitation is more energy-efficient (~2–5× power saving) but does not materially increase the softening magnitude (~1×), because softening is set by dislocation mobility once the breakaway threshold is crossed (NP_143). The fingerprint is therefore a diagnostic and a modest efficiency lever, not a magnitude lever.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_144"]),
                    new("Material control is an amplitude problem", "NP_145: material control is primarily an AMPLITUDE problem, not a frequency or waveform problem. Holding total energy constant, the five waveforms (sine/burst/chirp/noise/impulse train) differ only in peak amplitude and occupied bandwidth; there is no independent shape degree of freedom. Defect mobilization tracks peak amplitude: bursts and impulse trains mobilize defects more efficiently per unit energy precisely because they concentrate energy into a higher peak that crosses the breakaway threshold (NP_143) — the shock-loading result that plastic flow is driven by peak stress and duration, not wave shape. Dominance: amplitude > bandwidth > waveform. The optimal drive maximizes peak-per-energy while covering the defect band.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_145"]),
                    new("Softening energy goes into defect motion, not heat", "NP_146: the injected ultrasonic energy goes predominantly into DEFECT MOTION (mechanical/athermal), not heat. The three channels partition as elastic storage (small, recoverable), defect motion (dominant, recoverable — dislocations in metals, microcracks/contacts in rock), and heat (residual, via internal friction). The decisive evidence is Langenecker's result that the softening is NOT reproduced by the equivalent temperature rise — so it is athermal/mechanical, not thermal. Ordered metals (Al, steel) have the cleanest dislocation-dominated pathway; quartz/granite have more frictional/contact dissipation. This is why the yield stress drops far more than the modulus (NP_141).", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_146"]),
                    new("Resonance writes the defect score", "NP_147: coherent ultrasonic excitation is a TRUE defect-engineering tool, not only a transient softening lever. Beyond the reversible yield drop (NP_141/146), the four defect processes are motion (transient), creation, annihilation, and rearrangement (permanent). Sustained high-amplitude excitation permanently reduces dislocation density (annihilation), refines subgrains, and heals microcracks (crack-tip plasticity — e.g. copper 3× fatigue life), with residual hardening or softening depending on intensity. Metals are the richest medium; quartz/granite are crack/contact-limited. The defect fingerprint (NP_144) changes after conditioning — resonance is a 'write' operation on the defect score (NP_130).", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_147"]),
                    new("Material properties can be programmed", "NP_148: resonance control can become TRUE property programming (metal-centric), not simple softening. Target properties — hardness, yield stress, fatigue life, fracture toughness, damping — each map onto a specific defect lever (dislocation density, grain refinement, residual stress, crack closure). Parameter-controlled ultrasonic processes (shot peening; UNSM up to −1400 MPa compressive stress and fatigue limit up to 2×; USRP) move materials toward specified properties via directed, not random, defect evolution. Open-loop read→write→verify works today (NP_144 read, NP_147 write); closed-loop measure→excite→measure convergence is emerging (PARTIAL). Programming is metal-centric: ceramics, quartz, and granite lack the dislocation/grain levers and are only weakly programmable.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_148"]),
                    new("Materials have optimal defect states, under trade-offs", "NP_149: materials possess optimal defect topologies for specific properties, and resonance-driven defect engineering can tune toward them — but optimization is multi-objective. Each property (hardness, yield stress, fatigue life, fracture toughness, damping) has a distinct optimal defect state (density, topology, distribution) reached by directed (not random) evolution (NP_147/148). The optima conflict — hardness↔toughness (Hall–Petch), strength↔fatigue, strength↔damping (Ashby) — so the achievable states form a Pareto front, not a single point. The read→write→verify loop works open-loop today; the closed-loop optimize step is emerging (ML/Bayesian guidance).", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_149"]),
                    new("Property programming is fast — and already industrial", "NP_150: property programming is a PRACTICAL MANUFACTURING TECHNOLOGY, not a laboratory curiosity — for the surface channel. Defect motion and transient softening are µs–ms; permanent defect writing (annihilation, subgrain, crack healing) takes seconds–minutes; deep nanocrystallization tens of minutes. Ultrasonic peening / UNSM / USRP deliver surface hardness, residual stress, and fatigue-life gains (30–100%) in seconds–minutes — comparable or faster than shot peening (5–30 min) and far faster than annealing (hours) — and are already industrially deployed. The caveat: resonance control is surface-confined; it cannot reach bulk properties, so it replaces surface treatments, not heat treatment.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-NP_150"]),
                    new("The softening chain is known physics in AT vocabulary", "NP_151: of the NP_131–150 chain, essentially nothing experimentally supported requires Actualization Theory — the surviving results are standard condensed-matter/materials physics (athermal acoustic softening, soft modes, dislocation/defect mechanics, ultrasonic peening). AT contributed an INTERPRETATION (the 'critical mode / master key' is the Cochran soft mode re-labeled), a REFUTED prediction set (quantized 33/67/100% ladder NP_138, phase-coherence resource NP_142, THz NP_133), and one UNTESTED unique prediction (the fixed-T order-preserving large elastic dial NP_140). No confirmed new physics emerged from the chain. The honest separation: known physics dominates; AT = reinterpretation + refuted/untested predictions.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_151"]),
                    new("Defect memory is known physics, not a new capability", "NP_152: materials already function as programmable physical memory through defect engineering — KNOWN PHYSICS, industrialized as RRAM (oxygen-vacancy filaments), phase-change memory, FeRAM/MRAM domains, and NV-center spins, with nm-scale density, 10+ year retention, and 10⁴–10¹² rewrite cycles. The read→write→verify→erase cycle is daily operation. AT's 'writable resonance score' (NP_130) is an INTERPRETATION of this known capability; the specific resonance-driven (ultrasonic) rewritable memory is an AT QUESTION (surface-confined, no nm-scale addressability shown). No confirmed new capability.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_152"]),
                    new("Materials are systems with navigable state spaces", "NP_153: materials ARE systems with navigable state spaces, not objects with fixed properties — KNOWN PHYSICS. A material is a point in a high-dimensional defect/microstructure manifold (dislocation density, grain size, phase fraction, residual stress, cracks), navigated by thermal, mechanical, and ultrasonic operations; transitions are reversible or irreversible by path (anneal resets, cold work conditions). Materials science already navigates this space (phase diagrams, TTT/CCT, processing maps); property optimization is the extremum-search special case, state-space navigation the general frame (NP_149's Pareto front). Tune/train/condition/restore are standard metallurgy. AT's 'writable resonance score' is an INTERPRETATION; whether resonance adds a genuinely new navigation axis is an AT QUESTION.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_153"]),
                    new("Rock is contact-controlled, not defect-controlled", "NP_154: for granite-like materials, rigidity is controlled by GRAIN-CONTACT NETWORKS, not internal crystal defects — the dominant lever is CONTACT-NETWORK ENGINEERING, not defect engineering. Granite is a jammed granular system: rigidity is carried by a percolating force-chain network at grain contacts and microcracks, which dominate the brittle (low-T/P) regime; intragranular dislocations matter only in the semi-brittle/ductile regime at depth. Temporary softening occurs via contact-network disruption (acoustic fluidization), reducing friction/shear strength by a factor ~5–10 at microstrain and ~1–5 W/cm². NP_141–147's dislocation picture governs metals; rock is contact-controlled.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_154"]),
                    new("Force chains can be reconfigured", "NP_155: the force-chain network inside a granite-like material CAN be intentionally reconfigured — break, create, and redirect chains — and multiple stable contact-network states exist. Force-chain reconfiguration, directed anisotropy (chains align with load/shear direction), metastable jammed states (fragile/shear-jammed/ultra-stable), and granular memory/aging are KNOWN PHYSICS (DEM, photoelasticity, jamming phase diagrams). Reversible reconfiguration holds only below the damage threshold (sub-damage fabric rearrangement); above it, reconfiguration proceeds by microcracking — irreversible though chemistry is unchanged. So a loose granular mass is freely programmable between metastable states; a cemented granite block can be re-steered only within the reversible regime.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_155"]),
                    new("Force chains can be steered into preferred paths", "NP_156: force-chain networks can be deliberately steered into preferred load paths — selectively strengthening some chains while weakening others — via boundary-controlled loading, preload, and oriented vibration (DEM/photoelastic, rock-mechanics stress redistribution). This steers load concentration, stiffness anisotropy, and fracture location; it is the spatial version of NP_155 (steering WHERE stress flows). The open question is reversible, vibration-only steering of a CONSOLIDATED granite block, which is bounded by microcracking damage (NP_155).", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_156"]),
                    new("Resonance acts on organization, not on matter", "NP_157: in AT, resonant excitation acts on (B) the ORGANIZATION of the difference network, not directly on material properties. Because binding = phase-locking (NP_100), material properties are emergent readouts of the locking topology; resonance drives the locking/defect/contact network first and the derived properties change as a consequence (the σ_y ≫ E asymmetry of NP_141 is the signature). The proximal effect is reorganization — a reversible topology change (re-lock NP_132, contact re-arrange NP_155/156), a third category between elastic deformation and irreversible damage. Per NP_151/153, 'organization' is AT's word for the known defect/contact/microstructure state space, so the matter/organization distinction is an AT INTERPRETATION, not a new ontology.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_157"]),
                    new("Granite is a family of latent organizational states", "NP_158: granite behaves as a family of latent organizational states, not a single material — KNOWN PHYSICS. At fixed chemistry/mineral/temperature, a block possesses multiple metastable contact-network configurations (packing fraction, contact density, fabric orientation) selected by loading/vibration history (memory/aging); natural/compacted/vibrated/re-jammed states differ in stiffness, damping, and fracture behavior. Sub-damage transitions are reversible; damage-bounded ones are not (NP_155). AT's 'latent organizational state' is an INTERPRETATION of granular/jamming physics.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_158"]),
                    new("Granite can be structurally trained", "NP_159: granite-like materials CAN be structurally trained, not merely modified — KNOWN PHYSICS. Repeated preload/vibration/unload cycles accumulate a history-dependent fabric (granular memory/aging); the final contact-network state depends on the loading sequence, so a virgin block becomes anisotropic and develops a persistent preference for specific load paths. The trained block shows measurable differences in anisotropy, damping, fracture location, and force-chain orientation. AT's 'structural training' framing is an INTERPRETATION of this cyclic-shear/history physics (stress-force-fabric).", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_159"]),
                    new("Materials can hold an organizational field", "NP_160: a granite-like block CAN support spatially varying organizational states — a programmable internal organizational field — KNOWN PHYSICS. Different training histories write different regional states (localized compaction, peening depth profiles, oriented vibration), and distinct states coexist (hard compressed surface vs soft interior; shear bands), with spatially varying local stiffness, damping, and fracture response. The organizational state is a fabric FIELD φ(x) (fabric-tensor continuum), bounded by state diffusion, relaxation, and inter-region coupling. AT's 'organizational field' is an INTERPRETATION; fine-grained reversible field programming in consolidated rock is an AT QUESTION (damage-bounded, NP_155).", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_160"]),
                    new("Organizational change propagates as fronts", "NP_161: organizational change CAN propagate through a granite-like material as waves or fronts — KNOWN PHYSICS. Localized reorganization induces neighboring reorganization, producing propagating boundaries between organizational states: jamming fronts (at/above granular sound speed), compaction fronts, shear bands, fluidization fronts, and granular solitary waves — all distinct from ordinary elastic waves. Organization is therefore a DYNAMICAL FIELD φ(x,t), not a static state. Speed spans orders of magnitude, persistence is dissipation-limited, and reversibility is damage-bounded (NP_155). AT's 'organizational wave' is an INTERPRETATION of these known propagating fabric fronts.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_161"]),
                    new("The organizational field is directly imageable", "NP_162: the organizational field φ(x,t) CAN be directly imaged and tracked — KNOWN PHYSICS. Contact density, force chains, anisotropy, and local rigidity are measured directly by photoelasticity (force chains), X-ray microtomography (grain/contact), ultrasonic tomography (stiffness), and coda-wave/nonlinear interferometry (contact/crack evolution). Nonlinear acoustics + NCWI reveal contact/crack structure that linear property measurements average away (NP_137), so the organizational map is strictly finer than the material-property map. Real-time tracking is feasible (photoelastic video, near-real-time CWI); for a 1 m granite block, ultrasonic tomography + CWI/NCWI are standard.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_162"]),
                    new("The path matters more than the excitation", "NP_163: the path through organizational state space matters MORE than the instantaneous excitation — KNOWN PHYSICS. Granular/rock materials are athermal and non-ergodic: the final organizational state is a function of loading/vibration HISTORY, not the final load. Identical end conditions reached by different paths give different states (path A → X, path B → Y), differing in anisotropy, damping, and fracture behavior; unreachable states, hysteresis, and training loops (reversal memory, rejuvenation) are standard. So the true control variable is the TRAJECTORY, not vibration/stress/frequency alone. AT's framing is an INTERPRETATION of established granular memory/aging/hysteresis physics.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_163"]),
                    new("Organizational change can amplify itself", "NP_164: organizational change CAN amplify itself — KNOWN PHYSICS. In a jammed/contact network, a local rearrangement releases stress onto neighbors and lowers their barrier, so the change self-reinforces into cascades and avalanches (power-law, scale-free), distinct from non-amplifying fronts and elastic waves. The critical points are rigidity percolation, the jamming transition, and self-organized criticality; near them small inputs produce disproportionately large shifts. Amplification is bounded by the reversible–irreversible transition (NP_155). The NP_131 'percolation master key' is itself this known rigidity-percolation physics.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_164"]),
                    new("The periodic table is hosted hydrogenic content, not an AT derivation", "NP_166: the periodic table does NOT emerge from AT resonance organization alone. Its shell capacities 2, 8, 18, 32 = 2n² = 2·Σ(2l+1) are the hydrogenic (central-field Coulomb) degeneracy of the Schrödinger equation — imported QM content, the same structural obstruction as nuclear shells (NP_087/089: exact (2l+1) needs exact O(3), unavailable from the 1D/cubic D96). Electron shells are NOT resonance layers (D96 octave occupancy [4,4,87] ≠ 2n²), NOT organization levels (the NP_101 hierarchy stacks structures across scales, not intra-atom shells), and NOT locking capacities (binding explains that atoms exist, not shell occupation). Observed shells H [1], He [2], Li [2,1], Ne [2,8], Ar [2,8,8] are reproduced only by the hydrogenic filling; no AT-native count matches. Classification: atom binding + hierarchy DERIVED (NP_100/101); shells as AT objects REFUTED; capacities BOUNDARY-class (imported); the periodic table CORRESPONDENCE (NP_017 confirmed; NP_085 extended: chemistry is not derived); 'AT predicts 2, 8, 18, 32' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_166"]),
                    new("Unknown organization states exist as combinations of known axes, not as a new state of matter", "NP_167: there ARE physically allowed organization states humanity has never deliberately engineered — but every one is a combination/scale of known ordering physics, not a state needing new laws. Criterion B (plausible-but-unexplored) MET: long-range-ordered force-chain lattices, phononic structure in jammed contact networks, persistently driven jammed order, granular–topological hybrids — all expressible in ordinary condensed-matter terms. The 8 known organization classes (crystal, glass, liquid, granular, phononic, metamaterial, topological, force-chain) are all deliberately engineered; every AT ordering axis maps onto known physics (coherence → BECs/phase-locked arrays; force-chain order → granular force chains; resonance locking → mode-locked/synchronized oscillators; occupancy/actualization ordering → the defect/contact state space). The strongly AT-specific candidate — a phase ordered only in occupancy/actualization variables — has NO observable separable from ordinary ordered matter (NP_151 re-label; NP_157 no new ontology): criterion C (strong AT-specific) NOT met, SPECULATIVE at best. Resonance can move a material between organization states without chemistry change, reversibly sub-damage (NP_155/156), bounded by the small fixed-T dial (NP_140). Classification: known-state inventory CORRESPONDENCE; unexplored combinations EMERGENT; AT-only phase SPECULATIVE; 'no unknown states' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_167"]),
                    new("A finite graph gap is not the Yang–Mills mass gap", "NP_168: AT derives a positive dimensionless kinematic gap for the finite D96 circulant C_96(1..6): λ_gap = 0.386350893377790 and ω_gap = 0.621571309969974. But deterministic refinement gives λ_gap(N) = 4π²·91/N² + O(N^-4), so the unscaled graph gap tends to zero. Scaling by 1/a² at fixed compact circumference yields only the ordinary compact-domain Laplacian gap — not an infinite-volume interacting gauge-theory gap. QG244 supplies a classical/minimal Yang–Mills Lagrangian structure, not a rigorous quantum construction. AT still lacks the Hilbert/measure construction, Gauss-law/gauge-invariant sector, interacting Hamiltonian or transfer matrix, controlled regulator limit, R^4 infinite-volume limit, and uniform positive physical bound required by Clay. A graph-Laplacian mode is not a glueball; the 'mass-gap scale' label reused across QG161/169/171/246/260/262/300 is graph/operator nomenclature, not Clay evidence. Classification: finite-network gap DERIVED; regulator scaffold EMERGENT; 'AT solves the Clay Yang–Mills problem' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_168"]),
                    new("No natural AT scaling preserves a positive mass gap", "NP_169: given λ_gap(N) = 4π²·91/N² + O(N^-4), the limit of m(N) = f(N)·λ_gap(N) depends only on the growth of f = O(N^e): e<2 → ZERO, e=2 → POSITIVE, e>2 → DIVERGENT. Every existing AT primitive is O(1) or O(N) — occupancy (I_occ=0.7513, ln K=1.0986, ΩΛ=0.6839, K=3, Σocc=95, N), the D96 family (degree 12, Σd²=91, N), and the fixed-lattice-spacing physical scaling — so every AT-native scaling gives ZERO GAP. The only positive limits are (a) the imported external length L₀ of a fixed-compact-volume physical scaling (a kinematic finite-volume gap, not an infinite-volume mass gap) and (b) the tautological spectral-density renormalization ρ(λ_gap)·λ_gap→1 (Weyl law N(λ)≈2N√(λ/c), c=4π²·91). Ad-hoc f=N² is underivable (no AT primitive is Θ(N²)); f=N³ diverges. Classification: ZERO GAP for AT-native scalings; 'AT preserves a positive mass gap via a natural scaling' REFUTED.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_169"]),
                                        new("On a ring the spectrum IS the coupling", "T_001/T_002: on a circulant ring the Laplacian spectrum is the discrete Fourier transform of the edge-weight generating function, so a target spectrum → topology map is an inverse DFT that is exact and optimally robust. AT's D96 is the sparse, ALL-PHYSICAL solution — six positive edges, sitting deep inside the cone of negative-definite functions on Z_N, whose boundary is crossed exactly when the smallest generator coefficient w_d changes sign. Band-gap, clustered and extreme-octave spectra demand repulsive (negative) couplings and are unrealizable by a plain oscillator ring.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_001", "ResearchY-T_002"]),
                                        new("A general graph is not determined by its spectrum", "T_003: isospectral non-isomorphic graphs exist (none at n = 5, four at n = 6), so the spectral blueprint is a SPECIAL property of circulant (ring) structure, where the labeled spectrum is bijective with the coupling. This is precisely why D96 is a canonical attractor: its circulant geometry makes the spectrum a complete and unique description.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_003"]),
                                        new("Spectral rigidity is graded", "T_004: rigidity holds exactly (R = 1) for highly structured families (complete, path, cycle, grid, circulant, D96) and decays (R < 1) for looser families (bipartite, random sparse). D96 sits in the maximally rigid class — its circulant spectrum is a complete, unique, canonical description, consistent with its role as the canonical attractor of the theory.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_004"]),
                                        new("No few attractors dominate D96", "T_005: spectral organization COMPRESSES the attractor count (45 for D96, 13 in the 3D variant, vs ~96 for random), but does NOT by itself produce a single dominant attractor — D96's largest basin is ~6 %, because its octave/doublet degeneracy spreads the state space evenly. Strong dominance (D > 0.5) requires massive degeneracy (complete graph), and 'fewest attractors' can even favor unphysical clustered spectra. REFUTED: 'a few attractors dominate the D96 state space'.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-T_005"]),
                                        new("Dominance is Darwinian, not spectral", "T_006: dominant attractors are the product of resource COMPETITION, not of spectral structure alone. The model-free fitness w = m/λ drives every model to a single fittest eigenspace (D → 1, N_eff → 1), with the complete graph the degenerate exception. Spectral organization compresses the attractor count (T_005) and accelerates selection, but dominance itself is dynamical — this resolves the T_005 arc.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_006"]),
                                        new("Evolution on a spectral landscape has a finite species count", "T_007/T_008: diversity saturates to a stable mutation–selection balance that is independent of runtime and at or below the landscape size. Boundedness is DERIVED (finite landscape + positive mutation + crowding) and the saturation value is EMERGENT. The limit is S∞ = min(A, N_fit(μ, β, {w})): mutation widens the surviving cloud (∂S∞/∂μ ≥ 0), crowding levels the field (∂S∞/∂β ≥ 0), and landscape size is a ceiling S∞ does not track (∂S∞/∂A ≈ 0). Neither fitness variance nor spectral rigidity alone predicts S∞. REFUTED: mutation yields unbounded innovation.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_007", "ResearchY-T_008"]),
                                        new("The fitness reach is analytic only in the two solvable limits", "T_009: N_fit is analytically expressible at the crowding threshold (μ = 0) and at the Perron (linear) equilibrium (β = 0), both reproduced exactly by simulation, and the small-μ reach has a DERIVED geometric-tail form with EMERGENT coefficients. But the general (μ > 0, β > 0) interior has NO closed form, and a single scalar gap Δw is insufficient — N_fit depends on the full fitness spectrum.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-T_009"]),
                                        new("D96 is the compressed lattice", "T_010: D96 compresses survivors to ~5 because its circulant (highly degenerate) spectrum is SPARSE NEAR THE GAP, producing a peaked fitness field that the mutation–selection balance keeps to a handful of species; the random graph's dense near-degenerate spectrum produces a flat field that keeps ~17. The split is a DERIVED consequence of the spectral tail (via the exact β = 0 Perron and μ = 0 threshold laws); the precise value is EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_010"]),
                                        new("The historical ~19 species does not come from D96", "T_011: REFUTED — D96's spectral blueprint produces a species ceiling of ~5 (both as equilibrium survivors and as total discovery), by the exact T_008/T_009 laws S∞ = min(A, N_fit(μ, β, {w})) with D96's sparse, peaked fitness field (T_010). The value ~19 is instead nearest the RANDOM landscape's signature (17 survivors, 18 cumulative discoveries), implying a different landscape rather than AT's canonical D96.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-T_011"]),
                                        new("D96³ raises the species count to 16", "T_012: the cubic tensor product raises survivors from 5 to 16 (distance 3 from the historical ~19, within the legacy ±5 tolerance), a DERIVED effect of the 3D density of states, with 21 in the cubic 3-axis sector alone. D96³ therefore CORRESPONDS to the historical ~19 at order-of-magnitude level; the exact value 16 is EMERGENT and does not cleanly reproduce it.", TheoryBadge.Match, ["ResearchY-T_012"]),
                                        new("Compression is landscape size, not fitness peakedness", "T_013: D96³ compresses 20,811 → 16 because its 3D sum structure generates a huge landscape (A ≈ 20k distinct eigenvalues) while selection w = m/λ keeps only the near-gap modes (~16 of the 18 within 2λ₂). The compression ratio C = A/S∞ is DERIVED as a deterministic function of the spectrum, but its value (≈ 1300 for D96³, ~9 for D96, ~6 for random) is EMERGENT and dominated by A. REFUTED: fitness peakedness drives the compression — D96 is the most peaked spectrum yet compresses least.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-T_013"]),
                                        new("Near-gap density is exact counting", "T_014: N_gap(k) = Σ_{λ≤kλ₂} m(λ) is an exact count (DERIVED) whose k-growth follows the Weyl law of the tensor-product dimension (1D ∝ k^{1/2}, 3D ∝ k^{3/2}) and whose magnitude is set by the symmetry class — the degeneracy of λ₂ (2, 6, 1, 2, 32) and the octahedral orbit structure of the D96³ staircase (6, 12, 8, 6). D96 has only 2 modes / 1 distinct eigenvalue within 2λ₂; the specific values are EMERGENT.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_014"]),
                                        new("Spectral robustness is the degeneracy structure", "T_015: robustness is NOT controlled by the near-gap density ρ(k) nor by the gap size λ₂ — it is set by the DEGENERACY structure: the number of levels with multiplicity > 1 that split under a symmetry-breaking perturbation. This is a DERIVED function of the spectrum's multiplicity structure; the specific magnitudes are EMERGENT. It is the same multiplicity reservoir that D_047 quantifies as a divergent weak-ε gain, and it is why the D_048/D_049 adaptability axes are indexed by the degeneracy count.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-T_015", "ResearchY-D_047"]),
                                        new("D96 is not more robust than random", "T_015: the proposed robustness advantage of D96 is REFUTED — all three hypotheses (near-gap density ρ, gap size λ₂, and D96-vs-random superiority) are overturned, since robustness is governed by the degeneracy count instead. Under a symmetry-breaking perturbation D96 gives Δλ₂/λ₂ = 0.0077 with ΔA = 30, against random's 0.0003 with ΔA = 0 — different magnitudes, but not the claimed control-based ordering.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-T_015"]),
                                        new("The lock lattice is a proposal, not a result", "NP_170: the Ch8 lock law has only statistical evidence (datasets and synthetic cohorts, QG307–QG319), and AT's own Ch14 concedes that standard complexity measures match or beat the lock rule on an evolving cohort — so no further statistical audit can settle the question. The proposal is to test it as a DYNAMICAL PHASE TRANSITION on an engineered circulant C₉₆(1..6) ring: charged into a locked configuration, the state is predicted to retain longer than a degeneracy-matched control through the actualization barrier (QG120) and to be readable via Δθ interference (QG126). Pre-registered criteria: a physical-coupling threshold with sharpness ≥ 3 and width ≤ 0.4 (QG316); hysteresis area A > 0 at ≥ 5σ over ≥ 30 runs, absent in all three controls. STATUS: PLANNED — no experiment performed, no result claimed; it can fail to falsify AT or falsify the physical realizability of the lock, but it can never CONFIRM AT.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_170"]),
                                        new("The lock lattice is not an energy store", "NP_170: energy storage is explicitly abandoned — a 96-node lattice holds µJ-scale energy at kW-scale overhead if cryogenic, 10⁶–10⁹× behind Li-ion (0.9 MJ kg⁻¹, ~5000 cycles, ~€100 kWh⁻¹). What survives is a phase-memory / neuromorphic-threshold reading whose state variable is WHICH MODES ARE LOCKED, not how much charge is separated: the write is phase-pinning (M_002), so the write-energy floor is set by phase noise rather than Coulomb energy, with a target of < ~10 fJ/write and < 5 % variability. The locking nonlinearity (NP_005) and the engineered coupling network (NP_007/NP_011) are declared imported BOUNDARY inputs, and the retention hypothesis is an analogy awaiting test, not a derivation.", TheoryBadge.Postulated, ["ResearchY-NP_170"]),
                                        new("The proposal was revised against the post-fetch results", "NP_170 rev. 2: T_015 (robustness is degeneracy-controlled and 'D96 outperforms random' is REFUTED) makes a degeneracy-MATCHED control mandatory, scores R4 against it, ties the < 1 % spread tolerance to Δλ₂/λ₂ and ΔA, and forbids any superiority claim; T_010/T_012/T_014 (D96 is the compressed lattice — 5 survivors vs ~17 random, only 2 modes within 2λ₂, D96³ at 16) force the framing that D96 is chosen for DERIVABILITY, not capacity, with D96³ as a non-canonical engineering variant and the legacy ~19 unused; NP_169 (no AT-native scaling preserves a positive gap) forces the ring's nonzero gap to be declared an IMPORTED kinematic finite-size gap, so λ₂ is no longer described as a physical mass-gap scale; T_014 refines the protocol into per-mode ε (ε_j = 0.01 ω_j). Unchanged: the core experiment (R1–R5), the three reasons, the kill criteria, the density argument and every verified D96 number (λ₂ = 0.386351, ω₁ = 0.621571, span = 6.402515).", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_170", "ResearchY-T_015", "ResearchY-NP_169"]),
                                        new("The lock threshold is sharp and narrow — in the model", "NP_171 simulates NP_170's R3 protocol instead of performing it: the canonical C₉₆(±1..±6) ring under deterministic Adler/Kuramoto dynamics (imported nonlinearity, NP_005) with NO stochastic term, fixed-seed LCG initial phases, 30 seeds (= the protocol's ≥ 30 repeats) and metrics taken verbatim from §6 R3 / QG316. The transition is decisively SHARP — sharpness 23.69 ± 5.28 against a requirement of ≥ 3 — and NARROW — width 0.132 ± 0.083 against a limit of 0.4 — at g_c = 1.607 ± 0.030 (coupling in units of ω_max = 1). Two of the three pre-registered H1 criteria are therefore met by a wide margin. Classification: this is EMERGENT standard synchronization physics in an imported model, not new AT content.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_171"]),
                                        new("The pre-registered hysteresis criterion fails in the noiseless model", "NP_171: the hysteresis loop is real — at g = 1.6 the upward sweep gives f = 0.22 while the downward sweep still holds 0.62 — but its seed-to-seed scatter puts it at A = 0.0251 ± 0.0074, i.e. 3.4σ, below the pre-registered 5σ. H1 therefore FAILS for exactly one reason: the noiseless ideal ring cannot supply the bistability the hardware protocol demands, so a significant hardware hysteresis loop (if it appears) would be produced by the imported nonlinearity rather than by the lock structure. Harness: `AT.Core/Resonance/Kuramoto/LockLatticeSimulator.cs`, `AT.Tests/ResearchY/NP_NewPhysics/Y_NP_171_Tests.cs`.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_171"]),
                                        new("Nothing locks at the board's nominal maximum coupling", "NP_171: f(g = 1) = 0.0000 — at the coupling the Tier-1 board is normalized to reach (NP_170 §5.1) the pre-registered lock fraction is zero over the whole ramp.                                         The threshold lies at 1.61× that value, so the per-node lock term must reach K ≥ 6.394 rad/time = 1.61·ω_max = 10.29·ω₁ = 15.17·⟨|ω_i − Ω|⟩ (measured in the model: mean natural frequency Ω = 0.851 normalized, mean |ω_i − Ω| = 0.106 normalized). In the only transferable form (QG313 — the values are domain-specific and g* ≈ 0.31 is not a hardware number) the board must deliver a lock term ≈ 10.3× the fundamental frequency ω₁. A board dimensioned only to the nominal maximum would measure f(g) ≡ 0 and conclude, wrongly, that the lock law is absent.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_171", "ResearchY-NP_170"]),                                        new("The D96 lock lattice is not the easiest to lock", "NP_171 (consistent with T_015): the random-coupling control locks EARLIER than the canonical ring (g_c = 0.863 versus 1.607 — D96 needs ≈ 1.9× more coupling) and is the only lattice fully locked at g = 1, with EXACTLY zero hysteresis (A = 0.0000). The detuned control locks latest of the in-range lattices (1.986) with the largest hysteresis area (0.0342, but only 2.5σ). All four §6 controls fail H1 as §7 requires, but H1's existence test therefore does NOT discriminate the canonical lattice from its controls — the discriminating test must be R4 retention against the degeneracy-matched control.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_171", "ResearchY-T_015"]),
                                        new("The degeneracy pattern alone does not reproduce the threshold", "NP_171: the T_015 degeneracy-matched control — the D96 multiplicity pattern {1, 42×2, 5, 6} carried by non-D96 values — never locks inside the pre-registered range (f(3) = 0.167, so its transition is beyond g = 3), whereas the canonical ring saturates at g ≈ 2. The multiplicity structure is not sufficient for the lock transition; the frequency VALUES matter. Arrangement matters too: the canonical mode-index arrangement (g_c = 1.607) and the identical multiset sorted ascending (a monotone frequency gradient, 1.746) differ by 9%.", TheoryBadge.Partial, ["ResearchY-NP_171", "ResearchY-T_015"]),
                                        new("The near-gap doublet locks last, not first", "NP_171 (T_014 context): only the fundamental doublet sits within 2λ₂, so a near-gap-only lock would be a much weaker statement than a global one. In the model the near-gap pair {1, 95} locks at g = 1.688 — AFTER the global half-lock point (g_c = 1.607) — because its detuning from the collective frequency (ω₁ = 0.156 versus Ω = 0.851) is among the largest, and in Adler dynamics the largest detuning is captured last. The onset spread across all degeneracy classes is only 1.635–1.688, so the transition is collective rather than a sequence of isolated mode captures, and a near-gap lock is a distinct event from a global one — which is exactly why T_014 required both to be reported separately.", TheoryBadge.Compatible, ["ResearchY-NP_171", "ResearchY-T_014"]),
                                        new("ω_max is attained at k = 11/85, not k = 48", "NP_171 (label corrigendum, no value changes): NP_170 §3 records ω_max = √λ_max as '(k = 48, λ = 12)'. The numeric value 3.979620 is correct, but λ₄₈ = 12 exactly while the spectrum maximum is λ_max = 15.837372467 at k = 11 and its mirror k = 85. ω_max, the span 6.402515 and the octave bands [4,4,87] are all unaffected; the fastest mode is the 11/85 pair. Independently reproduced alongside λ₂ = 0.386350893377790, ω₁ = 0.621571309969974, 47 mirror pairs and 45 levels = 1 + 42×2 + 5 + 6.", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_171", "ResearchY-NP_170"]),
                                        new("Two-time-scale retention needs no barrier", "NP_172 (null audit of H2): five deterministic models — the D96 lock lattice, a random lattice, a high-Q linear ring and weakly/strongly nonlinear rings — are charged into the locked configuration and ringed down with NO barrier, no topological charge, no memory and no noise. The generic strongly nonlinear ring PASSES NP_170's H2 criterion (R = τ_slow/τ_fast = 10.248 ≥ 10) on amplitude-dependent (saturating) damping alone. The retention signature is therefore generic oscillator physics, not evidence for the AT-120 one-way barrier — which NP_170 §9 already labelled an analogy.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_172", "ResearchY-NP_170"]),
                                        new("The ring and a random lattice retain identically", "NP_172: at matched parameters (γ = 0.005, β = 0.05, g = 1.7) the D96 lock lattice gives τ_fast = 15.398, τ_slow = 97.901 → R = 6.358, and a random 12-regular lattice gives τ_fast = 13.665, τ_slow = 86.888 → R = 6.358 — IDENTICAL to four significant digits. The AT topology contributes nothing to the retention ratio, and at these parameters neither lattice reaches R ≥ 10. H2's discriminating expectation (only the locked lattice is two-time-scale, controls single-exponential) is refuted, and so is the value of R4 retention as a discriminator.", TheoryBadge.Falsified, ["ResearchY-NP_172", "ResearchY-NP_171"]),
                                        new("Retention is set by the saturation-to-damping ratio", "NP_172 (DERIVED, verified against its own ODE to < 1e−6 and against a coherent-ring simulation to 8.5e−4): for a coherent uniform-amplitude charge in a uniform-Q ring the ring-down envelope is exactly E(τ) = u₀·e^(−2γτ)/(1 + (β·u₀/γ)(1 − e^(−2γτ))). So the late-time rate is 2γ for EVERY saturation strength (τ_slow → 1/(2γ)), the early decay is accelerated by βu₀ (which is what makes the curve look two-time-scale), and the whole shape depends on the single dimensionless ratio β·u₀/γ — topology does not appear. With β = 0 the decay is single-exponential exactly, at any Q. β sweep: R = 1.06 (β = 0) → 2.927 (0.005) → 6.358 (0.05) → 13.811 (0.25, passes 10) → 10.248 (0.5).", TheoryBadge.Derived, ["ResearchY-NP_172"]),
